nd Water Flow Mitigation Rule (COA ECM 1.12.0 and COA Item No. 658S of the SSM) provision that all trenching greater than 5 feet deep must be inspected by a geologist (Texas P.G.) or a geologist's representative.\n    37→\n    38→## Block 7: notes — A block listing two benchmarks (B.M. #1 and B.M. #2) with descriptions indicating they are mag nails set in asphalt, along with their respective elevations in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n    39→\n    40→**Benchmarks**\n    41→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    42→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    43→\n    44→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    45→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    46→\n    47→## Block 12: text_block — A block providing the legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision.\n    48→\n    49→**Legal Description**\n    50→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    51→\n    52→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f0048680-e5a3-4ca8-8dfa-cc37e7cbf9e3","uuid":"d2159866-2551-4607-a0f6-59d169db2c1a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-21/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 21 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 2: diagram — A graphic scale bar showing distances of 0, 20, and 40 feet, along with the text 'Scale 1\" = 20''. A north arrow is also present above the scale bar.\n\n[Graphic scale bar showing distances 0, 20, 40]\nScale 1\" = 20'\n[North arrow pointing upwards and slightly to the right]\n\n## Block 3: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan showing multiple buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8) and their respective units, with FFE (Finished Floor Elevation) and square footage information. The plan includes electrical elements such as transformers, pull boxes, underground electric lines, and electrical meters. Additionally, the plan shows topography lines, property boundaries, and various easements like drainage, public utility, and wastewater. Information on adjoining properties is provided, detailing acreage, zoning, and use.\n\nA detailed site plan showing multiple buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8) and their respective units, with FFE (Finished Floor Elevation) and square footage information. The plan includes electrical elements such as transformers, pull boxes, underground electric lines, and electrical meters. Additionally, the plan shows topography lines, property boundaries, and various easements like drainage, public utility, and wastewater. Information on adjoining properties is provided, detailing acreage, zoning, and use.\n\n## Block 4: notes — This block contains 'Utility Notes' detailing contractor responsibilities regarding existing utilities and specific requirements for private domestic water and wastewater branches. Below the notes are disclaimers about the application not verifying all data and the reliance on record information for existing utilities.\n\n**Utility Notes**\n1. Contractor shall verify horizontal and vertical location of existing utilities prior to construction.\n2. Contractor shall verify location of existing line, point of connection and notify engineer of any conflicts/discrepancies prior to installation of improvements.\n3. All Private Domestic Water Branches to the buildings are to be 2\" HDPE service tube per Detail 520-AW-01B\n4. All Private Wastewater Branches to the building are to be 6\" PVC service connections per Detail 520-AW-01C\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n\n## Block 5: notes — A small block containing text that references another sheet for profile information.\n\nSee Sht. 19\nfor Profile\n\n## Block 6: notes — A block containing a 'Tree List' reference stating 'Refer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.' Below it is a 'Note' regarding a Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule that requires trenching over 5 feet deep to be inspected by a geologist.\n\n**Tree List**\nRefer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n\n**Note**\nThis project is subject to the Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule (COA ECM 1.12.0 and COA Item No. 658S of the SSM) provision that all trenching greater than 5 feet deep must be inspected by a geologist (Texas P.G.) or a geologist's representative.\n\n## Block 7: notes — A block listing two benchmarks (B.M. #1 and B.M. #2) with descriptions indicating they are mag nails set in asphalt, along with their respective elevations in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n\n**Benchmarks**\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 12: text_block — A block providing the legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision.\n\n**Legal Description**\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n","numLines":52,"startLine":1,"totalLines":52}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778187589559,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lz8WeBPfZvA54QuPH7wQwb","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 9 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: drawing — Notes detailing lighting requirements, specifically that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. It also includes illustrations of fully-shielded light fixtures.\n     4→\n     5→Lighting Note\n     6→All exterior lighting will be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. All site lighting to be located on the building will be in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5, and will be reviewed during building plan review. Any change or substitution of lams/light fixtures shall be submitted to the Director for approval in accordance with Section 2.5.2.E. See Figure 34.\n     7→[Illustration of four different fully-shielded light fixtures]\n     8→Figure 34\n     9→Examples of Fully-Shielded Light Fixtures\n    10→\n    11→## Block 2: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan for the Valley View Townhomes project. It shows the layout of multiple buildings, driveways, sidewalks, parking spaces, detention pond, retaining walls, utility lines, and easements. Key features include buildings numbered 1 through 8, various unit numbers, concrete sidewalks, decomposed granite walkways, a mail kiosk, and existing structures to be replaced or retained.\n    12→\n    13→Site Plan showing layout of buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8), driveways, parking, detention pond, utility lines, easements, existing trees, retaining walls, and surrounding properties.\n    14→\n    15→## Block 3: notes — A note specifying the required methods for installing fence post holes near a tree's Critical Root Zone (CRZ) to avoid damage, including manual digging and the application of hardwood mulch.\n    16→\n    17→Fence Note\n    18→Post holes that are to be installed near a trees Critical Root Zone (CRZ) will be dug by hand. No machinery is allowed in the CRZ and any work done in this area shall be done by hand. Add a 3-inch layer of hardwood mulch in the CRZ prior to commencing construction activities.\n    19→\n    20→## Block 4: notes — Notes regarding accessibility standards and requirements for the project, covering topics such as accessible parking, route cross-slopes, and ramp design.\n    21→\n    22→Accessibility Notes\n    23→1. Approval of these plans by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only. Compliance with accessibility standards such as the 2010 Standards for Accessible Design or the 2012 Texas Accessibility Standards was not verified. The applicant is responsible for compliance with all applicable accessibility standards.\n    24→2. Accessible parking spaces must be located on a surface with a slope not exceeding 1:50.\n    25→3. Accessible routes must have a cross-slope no greater than 1:50.\n    26→4. Slopes on accessible routes may not exceed 1:20 unless designed as a ramp.\n    27→5. The maximum slope of a ramp in new construction is 1:12. The maximum rise for any ramp is 20in. The maximum horizontal projection is 30 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:12 and 1:15, and 40 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:16 and 1:20.\n    28→\n    29→## Block 5: notes — A list of notes related to the release of the site plan, covering topics such as compliance with the approved plan, necessary permits, signage, easements, utility service, demolition, condominium regulations, driveway construction, and right-of-way excavation.\n    30→\n    31→a. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require site plan amendment and approval of the Development Services Department.\n    32→b. Approval of this Site Plan does not include Building and Fire Code approval nor building permit approval.\n    33→c. All signs must comply with requirements of the Land Development Code (Chapter 25-10).\n    34→d. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n    35→e. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n    36→f. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n    37→g. No certificate of occupancy may be issued for the proposed residential condominium project until the owner or owners of the property have complied with Chapter 81 and 82 of the Property Code of the State of Texas or any other statutes enacted by the State concerning condominiums.\n    38→h. For driveway construction: The owner is responsible for all costs for relocation of, or damage to utilities.\n    39→i. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n    40→\n    41→## Block 6: notes — General notes outlining the engineer's responsibilities, the limitations of the provided utility locations, and the contractor's obligation to verify these locations prior to construction.\n    42→\n    43→Release of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n    44→The location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n    45→\n    46→## Block 7: notes — Notes detailing regulations for trash collection on the property, including rules against leaving containers at the curbside unattended and the requirement for visible signage.\n    47→\n    48→Trash Collection Notes\n    49→Trash containers shall not be left at curb side unattended. Signs must be posted on the building or at a location that is publicly visible indicating that trash containers may not be left at curbside. The sign shall indicate the City of Austin's Code enforcement phone number for the public to report violations.\n    50→A restrictive covenant may be required for enforcement purposes.\n    51→Reference: TCM, 9.4.2\n    52→\n    53→## Block 8: notes — Information about two benchmarks used for the project, including their location (Mag Nail Set in Asphalt) and elevation in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n    54→\n    55→Benchmarks\n    56→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    57→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    58→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    59→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    60→\n    61→## Block 9: notes — The legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    62→\n    63→Legal Description\n    64→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    65→\n    66→## Block 10: notes — A table detailing the parking requirements and provisions for the project, including land use, parking spaces provided (surface/garage), compact spaces, ADA spaces, and bicycle spaces.\n    67→\n    68→Parking Data\n    69→Land Use Residential\n    70→Parking Spaces Provided 10 Surface/ 78 Garage\n    71→Compact Spaces Provided 0\n    72→ADA Spaces Required X\n    73→ADA Spaces Provided X\n    74→Total Bicycle Spaces Required 5\n    75→Long Term Bicycle Spaces Required 3\n    76→Long Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 39 (1 per Garage)\n    77→Short Term Bicycle Spaces Required 2\n    78→Short Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 2\n    79→\n    80→## Block 11: other — A graphic scale indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet on the site plan.\n    81→\n    82→0 20' 40'\n    83→Scale 1\" = 20'\n    84→\n    85→## Block 14: table — This table provides data on building heights, including the number of units, building type, average mean sea level (MSL), building height in feet, average building height (MSL), height allowed in feet, and height allowed (MSL).\n    86→\n    87→| Building # | Building Type | Average (MSL) | Building Height (FT) | Avg. Building Height (MSL) | Height Allowed (FT) | Height Allowed (MSL) |\n    88→| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n    89→| 1 | 4 Plex | 686.25 | 35' - 10.75\" | 722.15 | 40' | 726.25 |\n    90→| 2 | 6 Plex | 684.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.90 | 40' | 724.50 |\n    91→| 3 | 5 Plex | 685.00 | 36' - 1\" | 721.08 | 40' | 725.00 |\n    92→| 4 | 5 Plex | 684.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 724.25 |\n    93→| 5 | 4 Plex | 682.17 | 35' - 10.75\" | 718.07 | 40' | 722.17 |\n    94→| 6 | 4 Plex | 681.75 | 35' - 10.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 721.75 |\n    95→| 7 | 5 Plex | 682.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.90 | 40' | 722.50 |\n    96→| 8 | 6 Plex | 682.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.65 | 40' | 722.25 |\n    97→\n    98→## Block 15: table — This table provides data for various buildings in the project, including building type (e.g., 4 Plex, 6 Plex), gross floor area (sf), building coverage (sf), bedroom type, and number of units.\n    99→\n   100→| Building # | Building Type | Gross Floor Area (sf) | Building Coverage (sf) | Bedroom Type | Units |\n   101→| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n   102→| 1 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Two Bedroom | 16 |\n   103→| 2 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Three Bedroom | 23 |\n   104→| 3 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | Total Units | 39 |\n   105→| 4 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n   106→| 5 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Maximum Number of Building Stories = 3 | |\n   107→| 6 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | | |\n   108→| 7 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n   109→| 8 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Foundation = Slab on grade | |\n   110→\n   111→## Block 16: table — This table summarizes key site data, including zoning, use, site area, allowable and proposed impervious cover, total impervious cover, allowable and proposed building coverage, gross floor area, building slab construction, and common open space required and provided.\n   112→\n   113→| Site Data Table | | |\n   114→| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n   115→| Zoning: | MF-2/Multi-Family Low Density | |\n   116→| Use: Multi-Family Residential | s.f. | acres | % |\n   117→| Site Area: | 94,192 | 2.162 | |\n   118→| Allowable Impervious Cover: | 56,515 | 1.297 | 60% |\n   119→| Existing Impervious Cover: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n   120→| Impervious Cover Removed: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n   121→| Total Impervious Cover: | 55,502 | 1.274 | 59% |\n   122→| Allowable Building Coverage: | 47,096 | 1.081 | 50% |\n   123→| Proposed Building Coverage: | 32,296 | 0.741 | 34% |\n   124→| Gross Floor Area: | 83,986 | 1.928 | |\n   125→| Building Slab Construction | Slab On Grade | |\n   126→| Common Open Space Required | 4709.62 | |\n   127→| Common Open Space provided | 4710 | |\n   128→\n   129→## Block 17: table — This table shows the site area calculation for MF-2 zoning, detailing the number of units by efficiency, 1 bedroom, and 2+ bedroom, as well as required and provided site area.\n   130→\n   131→| Unit Type | Efficiency | 1 Bedroom | 2+ Bedroom |\n   132→| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n   133→| Number of Units | 0 | 0 | 39 |\n   134→| Required Site Area Per Unit (sf) | 1,600 | 2,000 | 2,400 |\n   135→| Site Area Required (sf) | 0 | 0 | 93,600 |\n   136→| Total Site Area Required (sf) | 93,600 | | |\n   137→| Total Site Area Provided (sf) | 94,192 | | |\n   138→\n   139→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f0048680-e5a3-4ca8-8dfa-cc37e7cbf9e3","uuid":"b9297a4e-92d1-4d9f-82bc-d410941aecdc","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-09/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 9 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: drawing — Notes detailing lighting requirements, specifically that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. It also includes illustrations of fully-shielded light fixtures.\n\nLighting Note\nAll exterior lighting will be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. All site lighting to be located on the building will be in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5, and will be reviewed during building plan review. Any change or substitution of lams/light fixtures shall be submitted to the Director for approval in accordance with Section 2.5.2.E. See Figure 34.\n[Illustration of four different fully-shielded light fixtures]\nFigure 34\nExamples of Fully-Shielded Light Fixtures\n\n## Block 2: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan for the Valley View Townhomes project. It shows the layout of multiple buildings, driveways, sidewalks, parking spaces, detention pond, retaining walls, utility lines, and easements. Key features include buildings numbered 1 through 8, various unit numbers, concrete sidewalks, decomposed granite walkways, a mail kiosk, and existing structures to be replaced or retained.\n\nSite Plan showing layout of buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8), driveways, parking, detention pond, utility lines, easements, existing trees, retaining walls, and surrounding properties.\n\n## Block 3: notes — A note specifying the required methods for installing fence post holes near a tree's Critical Root Zone (CRZ) to avoid damage, including manual digging and the application of hardwood mulch.\n\nFence Note\nPost holes that are to be installed near a trees Critical Root Zone (CRZ) will be dug by hand. No machinery is allowed in the CRZ and any work done in this area shall be done by hand. Add a 3-inch layer of hardwood mulch in the CRZ prior to commencing construction activities.\n\n## Block 4: notes — Notes regarding accessibility standards and requirements for the project, covering topics such as accessible parking, route cross-slopes, and ramp design.\n\nAccessibility Notes\n1. Approval of these plans by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only. Compliance with accessibility standards such as the 2010 Standards for Accessible Design or the 2012 Texas Accessibility Standards was not verified. The applicant is responsible for compliance with all applicable accessibility standards.\n2. Accessible parking spaces must be located on a surface with a slope not exceeding 1:50.\n3. Accessible routes must have a cross-slope no greater than 1:50.\n4. Slopes on accessible routes may not exceed 1:20 unless designed as a ramp.\n5. The maximum slope of a ramp in new construction is 1:12. The maximum rise for any ramp is 20in. The maximum horizontal projection is 30 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:12 and 1:15, and 40 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:16 and 1:20.\n\n## Block 5: notes — A list of notes related to the release of the site plan, covering topics such as compliance with the approved plan, necessary permits, signage, easements, utility service, demolition, condominium regulations, driveway construction, and right-of-way excavation.\n\na. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require site plan amendment and approval of the Development Services Department.\nb. Approval of this Site Plan does not include Building and Fire Code approval nor building permit approval.\nc. All signs must comply with requirements of the Land Development Code (Chapter 25-10).\nd. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\ne. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\nf. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\ng. No certificate of occupancy may be issued for the proposed residential condominium project until the owner or owners of the property have complied with Chapter 81 and 82 of the Property Code of the State of Texas or any other statutes enacted by the State concerning condominiums.\nh. For driveway construction: The owner is responsible for all costs for relocation of, or damage to utilities.\ni. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n\n## Block 6: notes — General notes outlining the engineer's responsibilities, the limitations of the provided utility locations, and the contractor's obligation to verify these locations prior to construction.\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n\n## Block 7: notes — Notes detailing regulations for trash collection on the property, including rules against leaving containers at the curbside unattended and the requirement for visible signage.\n\nTrash Collection Notes\nTrash containers shall not be left at curb side unattended. Signs must be posted on the building or at a location that is publicly visible indicating that trash containers may not be left at curbside. The sign shall indicate the City of Austin's Code enforcement phone number for the public to report violations.\nA restrictive covenant may be required for enforcement purposes.\nReference: TCM, 9.4.2\n\n## Block 8: notes — Information about two benchmarks used for the project, including their location (Mag Nail Set in Asphalt) and elevation in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 9: notes — The legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 10: notes — A table detailing the parking requirements and provisions for the project, including land use, parking spaces provided (surface/garage), compact spaces, ADA spaces, and bicycle spaces.\n\nParking Data\nLand Use Residential\nParking Spaces Provided 10 Surface/ 78 Garage\nCompact Spaces Provided 0\nADA Spaces Required X\nADA Spaces Provided X\nTotal Bicycle Spaces Required 5\nLong Term Bicycle Spaces Required 3\nLong Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 39 (1 per Garage)\nShort Term Bicycle Spaces Required 2\nShort Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 2\n\n## Block 11: other — A graphic scale indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet on the site plan.\n\n0 20' 40'\nScale 1\" = 20'\n\n## Block 14: table — This table provides data on building heights, including the number of units, building type, average mean sea level (MSL), building height in feet, average building height (MSL), height allowed in feet, and height allowed (MSL).\n\n| Building # | Building Type | Average (MSL) | Building Height (FT) | Avg. Building Height (MSL) | Height Allowed (FT) | Height Allowed (MSL) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | 4 Plex | 686.25 | 35' - 10.75\" | 722.15 | 40' | 726.25 |\n| 2 | 6 Plex | 684.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.90 | 40' | 724.50 |\n| 3 | 5 Plex | 685.00 | 36' - 1\" | 721.08 | 40' | 725.00 |\n| 4 | 5 Plex | 684.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 724.25 |\n| 5 | 4 Plex | 682.17 | 35' - 10.75\" | 718.07 | 40' | 722.17 |\n| 6 | 4 Plex | 681.75 | 35' - 10.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 721.75 |\n| 7 | 5 Plex | 682.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.90 | 40' | 722.50 |\n| 8 | 6 Plex | 682.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.65 | 40' | 722.25 |\n\n## Block 15: table — This table provides data for various buildings in the project, including building type (e.g., 4 Plex, 6 Plex), gross floor area (sf), building coverage (sf), bedroom type, and number of units.\n\n| Building # | Building Type | Gross Floor Area (sf) | Building Coverage (sf) | Bedroom Type | Units |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Two Bedroom | 16 |\n| 2 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Three Bedroom | 23 |\n| 3 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | Total Units | 39 |\n| 4 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n| 5 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Maximum Number of Building Stories = 3 | |\n| 6 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | | |\n| 7 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n| 8 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Foundation = Slab on grade | |\n\n## Block 16: table — This table summarizes key site data, including zoning, use, site area, allowable and proposed impervious cover, total impervious cover, allowable and proposed building coverage, gross floor area, building slab construction, and common open space required and provided.\n\n| Site Data Table | | |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Zoning: | MF-2/Multi-Family Low Density | |\n| Use: Multi-Family Residential | s.f. | acres | % |\n| Site Area: | 94,192 | 2.162 | |\n| Allowable Impervious Cover: | 56,515 | 1.297 | 60% |\n| Existing Impervious Cover: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n| Impervious Cover Removed: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n| Total Impervious Cover: | 55,502 | 1.274 | 59% |\n| Allowable Building Coverage: | 47,096 | 1.081 | 50% |\n| Proposed Building Coverage: | 32,296 | 0.741 | 34% |\n| Gross Floor Area: | 83,986 | 1.928 | |\n| Building Slab Construction | Slab On Grade | |\n| Common Open Space Required | 4709.62 | |\n| Common Open Space provided | 4710 | |\n\n## Block 17: table — This table shows the site area calculation for MF-2 zoning, detailing the number of units by efficiency, 1 bedroom, and 2+ bedroom, as well as required and provided site area.\n\n| Unit Type | Efficiency | 1 Bedroom | 2+ Bedroom |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Number of Units | 0 | 0 | 39 |\n| Required Site Area Per Unit (sf) | 1,600 | 2,000 | 2,400 |\n| Site Area Required (sf) | 0 | 0 | 93,600 |\n| Total Site Area Required (sf) | 93,600 | | |\n| Total Site Area Provided (sf) | 94,192 | | |\n\n","numLines":139,"startLine":1,"totalLines":139}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778187589561,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013CHvKvSTcFPNuBN2GJRsWE","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 1 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: drawing — This block shows a map of the project location, highlighting the surrounding streets and intersection. A pointer indicates the specific project location.\n     4→\n     5→[Map showing the project location and surrounding streets.]\n     6→\n     7→N\n     8→\n     9→PROJECT\n    10→LOCATION\n    11→\n    12→## Block 5: table — This block contains data related to the Austin Fire Department's requirements for the project. It includes information such as fire design codes, fire flow demand, occupancy classification, construction classification, building fire area, automatic fire sprinkler system type, reduced fire flow demand, applies to project status, AFD fire hydrant flow test dates and locations, city of Austin pipeline ordinance compliance, high-rise status, international wildland-urban interface code compliance, and alternative method of compliance AMOC.\n    13→\n    14→Austin Fire Department\n    15→\n    16→| Fire Design Codes | 2021 International Fire Code Edition\n    17→with City of Austin Local Amendments |\n    18→|---|---|\n    19→| Fire Flow Demand @ 20 psi\n    20→(gpm) | 3000 GPM |\n    21→| Occupancy Classification | R3 |\n    22→| Construction Classification | Type V-B |\n    23→| Building Fire Area (s.f.) | 13,344 |\n    24→| Automatic Fire Sprinkler System\n    25→Type (If applicable) | NFPA 13D |\n    26→| Reduced Fire Flow Demand @ 20\n    27→psi for having a sprinkler system\n    28→(gpm) | 1500 GPM |\n    29→| AFD Fire Hydrant Flow Test Date | 3/21/2025 |\n    30→| AFD Fire Hydrant Flow Test\n    31→Location | 3700 Valley View Rd. |\n    32→| City of Austin Pipeline Ordinance\n    33→Applies to Project | No |\n    34→| High-Rise | No |\n    35→| International Wildland-Urban\n    36→Interface Code | N/A |\n    37→| Alternative Method of\n    38→Compliance AMOC (If applicable) | N/A |\n    39→\n    40→## Block 6: table — This block contains the site development data for the Valley View Townhomes project. It includes contact information for the owner, engineer, architect, and landscape architect, as well as the legal description, subdivision file number, zoning case number, and limits of construction.\n    41→\n    42→Site Development Data Valley View Townhomes\n    43→Owner: WM 41 Austin RE, LLC.\n    44→Contact Person: Richard D. Agnew\n    45→500 West 5th Street, Ste. 1010\n    46→Austin, Texas 78701\n    47→Phone: 512-565-6159\n    48→Email: Richard.Agnew@slpops.com\n    49→\n    50→Engineer: Bleyl Engineering\n    51→Contact Person: Jason Rodgers\n    52→7701 San Felipe, Suite 200\n    53→Austin, Texas 78729\n    54→Phone: (512) 454-2400\n    55→Email: jrodgers@bleylengineering.com\n    56→\n    57→Architect: Danze & Davis Architects, Inc.\n    58→Contact Person: Harrison Davis\n    59→4701 Spicewood Springs Rd, Ste 200\n    60→Austin, Texas 78759\n    61→Phone: (512) 343-0714\n    62→\n    63→Landscape Architect: Blu Fish Collaborative, Inc.\n    64→Contact Person: Mike Fishbaugh\n    65→3607 S. Lamar Blvd., Suite 104\n    66→Austin, Texas 78704\n    67→Phone: (512) 338-4115\n    68→\n    69→Legal Description: Lot 1 Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County,\n    70→Texas\n    71→2.16 Acres\n    72→Subdivision File No. C8S-75-160\n    73→Zoning Case No. C14-2011-0093\n    74→Limits of Construction: 2.26 Acres\n    75→\n    76→## Block 8: text_block — This block is the title and primary identification information for the site plan. It states the project name, address, jurisdiction, SP number, and submittal date.\n    77→\n    78→Site Plan\n    79→Valley View Townhomes\n    80→3759 Valley View Road\n    81→Austin, Texas 78704\n    82→Jurisdiction: Austin Full Purpose\n    83→\n    84→SP-2025-0126C\n    85→Submittal Date: May 20, 2025\n    86→\n    87→## Block 9: text_block — This block is a disclaimer from Bleyl Engineering stating that they are not responsible for the accuracy of the survey or design errors resulting from survey inaccuracies.\n    88→\n    89→Bleyl Engineering and its associates will not be held responsible for the accuracy of the survey or for design errors or\n    90→omissions resulting from survey inaccuracies.\n    91→\n    92→## Block 10: text_block — This block is a note stating that all detention basins, water quality ponds, and appurtenances must be maintained by the record owner in accordance with the Drainage and Environmental Criteria Manual.\n    93→\n    94→All detention basins, water quality ponds and appurtenances which receive stormwater runoff from commercial or\n    95→multi-family development shall be maintained by the record owner in accordance with the maintenance standards in the\n    96→Drainage and Environmental Criteria Manual.\n    97→\n    98→## Block 11: text_block — This block is a note stating that compliance with the Commercial and Multi-Family Recycling Ordinance is mandatory.\n    99→\n   100→Compliance with the Commercial and Multi-Family Recycling Ordinance is mandatory for multi-family complexes,\n   101→businesses and office buildings.\n   102→\n   103→## Block 12: text_block — This block is a note indicating where the Integrated Pest Management Plan can be found in the Official Public Records of Travis County, Texas.\n   104→\n   105→For Integrated Pest Management Plan, see agreement filed in document No.____________, Official Public Records,\n   106→Travis County, Texas.\n   107→\n   108→## Block 13: text_block — This block is a note stating that the project is located within the West Bouldin Creek Watershed and that no portion of the tract is within the 100-year flood plain.\n   109→\n   110→This project is located within the West Bouldin Creek Watershed (Urban). All storm flows from this site will be directed\n   111→to the West Bouldin Creek Watershed. No portion of this tract is within the boundaries of the 100 year flood plain of\n   112→any waterway that is within the limits of study of the Federal Flood Insurance Administration FIRM panel\n   113→#48453C0585H, dated September 26, 2008 for Travis County.\n   114→\n   115→## Block 14: text_block — This block is a note stating that the project is not located within the Edwards Aquifer Recharge Zone or the Edwards Aquifer Contributing Zone.\n   116→\n   117→Edwards Aquifer Note This project is not located within the Edwards Aquifer Recharge Zone or the Edwards Aquifer\n   118→Contributing Zone.\n   119→\n   120→## Block 15: text_block — This block contains the scale, mapsco number, and city of Austin grid number for the project location map.\n   121→\n   122→Project Location Map\n   123→Scale: 1\" = 400'\n   124→Mapsco #614S\n   125→City of Austin Grid #G19\n   126→\n   127→## Block 16: text_block — This block contains a table for recording the dates that various City departments reviewed the plans.\n   128→\n   129→Reviewed By:\n   130→\n   131→Development Services Department Date\n   132→SP-2025-0126C\n   133→Development Permit No.\n   134→\n   135→City of Austin Fire Department Date\n   136→\n   137→Austin Water Date\n   138→\n   139→## Block 17: text_block — This block is a note indicating that the contractor is responsible for filing all necessary forms with the Environmental Protection Agency for projects involving 5 acres or more of disturbed area.\n   140→\n   141→Contractor is responsible for filing all necessary forms with the Environmental Protection Agency for all projects\n   142→involving 5 acres or more of disturbed area or part of a larger development which will eventually disturb 5 acres or more.\n   143→Note The contractor and the owner both must file a Notice of Intent.\n   144→\n   145→## Block 18: text_block — This block is a note requiring all potable water system components installed after January 4, 2014, to be \"lead free\" according to the US Safe Drinking Water Act. It lists examples of exempt and non-exempt components.\n   146→\n   147→All potable water system components installed after January 4, 2014, shall be essentially \"lead free\" according to the\n   148→US Safe Drinking Water Act. Examples are valves (corporation stop, curb stop, and pressure reducing), nipples,\n   149→bushings, pipe, fittings and backflow preventers. Fire hydrants, tapping saddles and 2 inch and larger gate valves are\n   150→the only components exempt from this requirement. Components that are not clearly identified by the manufacturer as\n   151→meeting this requirement either by markings on the component or on the packaging shall not be installed.\n   152→\n   153→## Block 19: text_block — This block is a note regarding the project's private hydrants and the owner's responsibility to comply with the Austin Fire Code. It states that private hydrants must be serviced, maintained, and flowed annually.\n   154→\n   155→City of Austin - Water and Wastewater Utility - Special Services Division (512) 972-1060\n   156→This project has private hydrants located within the property. The property owner is required to comply with Austin\n   157→Fire Code. Failure to comply may result in civil and/or criminal remedies available to the City. The performance of\n   158→this obligation shall always rest with the owner or record. Fire hydrants on private property are required to serviced,\n   159→maintained and flowed annually, using a contractor registered with the City to provide this service. This project\n   160→includes __1__ private hydrants.\n   161→\n   162→## Block 20: text_block — This block states that a civil engineer registered in Texas must certify the plan as complete, accurate, and in compliance with the Land Development Code, unless waived by the director.\n   163→\n   164→A civil engineer registered in Texas must certify a plan or plat as complete, accurate, and in compliance with Chapter\n   165→25-8 Subchapter A of the Land Development Code. The director may waive this requirement after making a\n   166→determination that the plan or plat includes only minor alterations or improvement that do not require the services of an\n   167→engineer.\n   168→\n   169→## Block 21: text_block — This block is a note regarding the placement of silt fences and additional erosion/sedimentation controls as required by the City's Environmental and Conservation Services Department. It also specifies that only approved construction entrances should be used.\n   170→\n   171→All temporary spoils and equipment storage areas shall have silt fence placed along the perimeter of the downslope\n   172→side. Additional erosion/sedimentation controls may be required at the direction of the City's Environmental and\n   173→Conservation Services Department Officer/Inspector. The contractor shall work closely with all City Personnel to insure\n   174→adequacy of placement and maintenance of all erosion/sedimentation control devices. Only those construction\n   175→entrances shown on the approved site plan shall be used for ingress and egress to and from site. In the event that\n   176→additional entrance locations are required to facilitate placement of materials, the contractor shall construct a stabilized\n   177→construction entrance in accordance with City of Austin Standard Details in order to comply with all provisions of the\n   178→City's Land Development Code and Environmental Protection Ordinances.\n   179→\n   180→## Block 22: text_block — This block is a note stating that approval by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only and that approval by other governmental entities may be required.\n   181→\n   182→Approval of these plans by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only. Approval by\n   183→other governmental entities may be required prior to the start of construction. The applicant is responsible for\n   184→determining what additional approvals may be necessary.\n   185→\n   186→## Block 23: text_block — This block is a note stating that the project received approval from the Watershed Protection Department for a waiver to a specific section of the COA Drainage Criteria Manual.\n   187→\n   188→DCM Waiver Note: On 01/21/2026 this project received approval from the Watershed Protection Department of a\n   189→waiver to Section 1.2.4.E.1.e.i of the COA Drainage Criteria Manual.\n   190→\n   191→## Block 24: text_block — This block contains a stamp for the City of Austin to officially approve the site plan. It includes fields for the file number, application date, approval date, whether it was approved administratively or by commission, the expiration date, case manager, and zoning.\n   192→\n   193→SITE PLAN APPROVAL SHEET ____1____ of ____39____\n   194→FILE NUMBER ____SP-2025-0126C____ APPLICATION DATE ____05/20/2025____\n   195→APPROVED ON _________________ \n   196→□ADMINISTRATIVELY \n   197→□BY COMMISSION\n   198→UNDER SECTION 112 OF CHAPTER 25-5 OF THE CITY OF AUSTIN CODE.\n   199→EXPIRATION DATE (25-5-81,LDC) ___________________ CASE MANAGER ___Jim Gobel_______\n   200→____________________________________________________________________________________________\n   201→Austin Development Services\n   202→RELEASED FOR GENERAL COMPLIANCE _________________ ZONING ____MF-2____\n   203→____________________________________________________________________________________________\n   204→Approval Stamps:\n   205→\n   206→## Block 25: text_block — This block contains a certification statement signed by the engineer, stating that the engineering work complies with the Texas Engineering Practice Act and the City of Austin's Land Development Code.\n   207→\n   208→I Jason K. Rodgers do hereby certify that the engineering work being submitted herein complies with all provisions of\n   209→the Texas Engineering Practice Act, including Section 131.152(e). I hereby acknowledge that any misrepresentation\n   210→regarding this certification constitutes a violation of the Act, and may result in criminal, civil and/or administrative\n   211→penalties against me, as authorized by the Act. The plan or plat is complete, accurate and in compliance with Chapter\n   212→25-8 Subchapter A of the Land Development Code.\n   213→I certify that these engineering documents are complete, accurate and adequate for the intended purposes,\n   214→including construction, but are not authorized for construction prior to formal City approval.\n   215→\n   216→[Signature of Jason K. Rodgers]\n   217→Jason K. Rodgers\n   218→2/27/26\n   219→\n   220→## Block 26: text_block — This block contains notes regarding the requirement for a temporary traffic control plan (TCP) to be reviewed and approved prior to construction.\n   221→\n   222→Traffic Control Plan Note:\n   223→This note is being placed on the plan set in the absence of a temporary traffic control plan (TCP) with the full\n   224→understanding that an engineered TCP shall be reviewed and approved by the Right of Way Management\n   225→Division. Furthermore, a TCP shall be submitted to TCPReview@austintexas.gov for review a minimum of 6\n   226→weeks prior to the start of construction. The applicant/project representative further recognizes that a TCP review\n   227→fee is required for the initial review and all re- reviews, as prescribed by the most current version of the City's fee\n   228→ordinance.\n   229→\n   230→The following must be taken into consideration:\n   231→· Refer to the “Mobility Guidelines” for developing traffic control strategies\n   232→http://www.austintexas.gov/page/mobility-guidelines.\n   233→· TCPs shall not be approved without an approved site plan, site plan exemption, or general permit.\n   234→· A traffic control plan is not a permit.\n   235→\n   236→## Block 27: text_block — This block is a note stating that the site plan is subject to Subchapter E of the Land Development Code (Commercial Design Standards) and that compliance is required during building code review.\n   237→\n   238→This site plan is subject to Subchapter E of the Land Development Code (Commercial Design Standards). Compliance\n   239→with Building Design Standards, Article 3 of Subchapter E, is required, and is to be reviewed for compliance during\n   240→building code review.\n   241→\n   242→## Block 28: text_block — This block states that a fee in-lieu of parkland dedication must be paid prior to the issuance of the Certificate of Occupancy.\n   243→\n   244→A fee in-lieu of parkland dedication must be paid for 39 residential units prior to the issuance of the Certificate of\n   245→Occupancy. No Certificate of Occupancy may be issued for this development until receipt of payment. Contact the\n   246→Parks and Recreation Department prior to requesting inspections.\n   247→\n   248→## Block 29: text_block — This block is a disclaimer stating that the City's review of the application does not verify the data, information, and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the submittal's completeness, accuracy, and adequacy.\n   249→\n   250→Release of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the\n   251→applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her\n   252→submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n   253→\n   254→## Block 30: text_block — This block is a note stating that development of structures requiring a building permit must comply with the City of Austin Street Impact Fee Ordinances and must be paid upon completion of the building permit plan review.\n   255→\n   256→Development of structures that require a building permit within this Site Plan, or revisions thereof, are required to\n   257→comply with the City of Austin Street Impact Fee Ordinances, as applicable, and must be paid upon completion of the\n   258→building permit plan review for each building. Reference: LDC 25-6-662\n   259→\n   260→## Block 31: text_block — This block is a note regarding procedures to follow if an underground storage tank (UST) is found during construction.\n   261→\n   262→If at any time during construction of this project as underground storage tank (UST) is found, construction in that area\n   263→must stop until a City of Austin UST Construction Permit is applied for and approved. Any UST removal work must be\n   264→conducted by a UST Contractor that is registered with the Texas Commission on Environmental Quality (TCEQ).\n   265→Contact UST@austintexas.gov if you have any questions.[COA Title 6]\n   266→\n   267→## Block 32: text_block — This block is a note stating that the engineer who prepared the plans is responsible for their adequacy and that the City of Austin relies on their work.\n   268→\n   269→All responsibility for the adequacy of these plans remains with the Engineer who prepared them. In reviewing these\n   270→plans, the City of Austin must rely upon the adequacy of the work of the Design Engineer.\n   271→\n   272→## Block 33: text_block — This block is a note stating that the proposed stacked biofiltration/detention pond will be privately maintained.\n   273→\n   274→The proposed stacked biofiltration/detention pond will\n   275→be privately maintained.\n   276→\n   277→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f0048680-e5a3-4ca8-8dfa-cc37e7cbf9e3","uuid":"1b5701af-4bcf-43a2-9631-719d7cfee8e5","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-01/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 1 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: drawing — This block shows a map of the project location, highlighting the surrounding streets and intersection. A pointer indicates the specific project location.\n\n[Map showing the project location and surrounding streets.]\n\nN\n\nPROJECT\nLOCATION\n\n## Block 5: table — This block contains data related to the Austin Fire Department's requirements for the project. It includes information such as fire design codes, fire flow demand, occupancy classification, construction classification, building fire area, automatic fire sprinkler system type, reduced fire flow demand, applies to project status, AFD fire hydrant flow test dates and locations, city of Austin pipeline ordinance compliance, high-rise status, international wildland-urban interface code compliance, and alternative method of compliance AMOC.\n\nAustin Fire Department\n\n| Fire Design Codes | 2021 International Fire Code Edition\nwith City of Austin Local Amendments |\n|---|---|\n| Fire Flow Demand @ 20 psi\n(gpm) | 3000 GPM |\n| Occupancy Classification | R3 |\n| Construction Classification | Type V-B |\n| Building Fire Area (s.f.) | 13,344 |\n| Automatic Fire Sprinkler System\nType (If applicable) | NFPA 13D |\n| Reduced Fire Flow Demand @ 20\npsi for having a sprinkler system\n(gpm) | 1500 GPM |\n| AFD Fire Hydrant Flow Test Date | 3/21/2025 |\n| AFD Fire Hydrant Flow Test\nLocation | 3700 Valley View Rd. |\n| City of Austin Pipeline Ordinance\nApplies to Project | No |\n| High-Rise | No |\n| International Wildland-Urban\nInterface Code | N/A |\n| Alternative Method of\nCompliance AMOC (If applicable) | N/A |\n\n## Block 6: table — This block contains the site development data for the Valley View Townhomes project. It includes contact information for the owner, engineer, architect, and landscape architect, as well as the legal description, subdivision file number, zoning case number, and limits of construction.\n\nSite Development Data Valley View Townhomes\nOwner: WM 41 Austin RE, LLC.\nContact Person: Richard D. Agnew\n500 West 5th Street, Ste. 1010\nAustin, Texas 78701\nPhone: 512-565-6159\nEmail: Richard.Agnew@slpops.com\n\nEngineer: Bleyl Engineering\nContact Person: Jason Rodgers\n7701 San Felipe, Suite 200\nAustin, Texas 78729\nPhone: (512) 454-2400\nEmail: jrodgers@bleylengineering.com\n\nArchitect: Danze & Davis Architects, Inc.\nContact Person: Harrison Davis\n4701 Spicewood Springs Rd, Ste 200\nAustin, Texas 78759\nPhone: (512) 343-0714\n\nLandscape Architect: Blu Fish Collaborative, Inc.\nContact Person: Mike Fishbaugh\n3607 S. Lamar Blvd., Suite 104\nAustin, Texas 78704\nPhone: (512) 338-4115\n\nLegal Description: Lot 1 Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County,\nTexas\n2.16 Acres\nSubdivision File No. C8S-75-160\nZoning Case No. C14-2011-0093\nLimits of Construction: 2.26 Acres\n\n## Block 8: text_block — This block is the title and primary identification information for the site plan. It states the project name, address, jurisdiction, SP number, and submittal date.\n\nSite Plan\nValley View Townhomes\n3759 Valley View Road\nAustin, Texas 78704\nJurisdiction: Austin Full Purpose\n\nSP-2025-0126C\nSubmittal Date: May 20, 2025\n\n## Block 9: text_block — This block is a disclaimer from Bleyl Engineering stating that they are not responsible for the accuracy of the survey or design errors resulting from survey inaccuracies.\n\nBleyl Engineering and its associates will not be held responsible for the accuracy of the survey or for design errors or\nomissions resulting from survey inaccuracies.\n\n## Block 10: text_block — This block is a note stating that all detention basins, water quality ponds, and appurtenances must be maintained by the record owner in accordance with the Drainage and Environmental Criteria Manual.\n\nAll detention basins, water quality ponds and appurtenances which receive stormwater runoff from commercial or\nmulti-family development shall be maintained by the record owner in accordance with the maintenance standards in the\nDrainage and Environmental Criteria Manual.\n\n## Block 11: text_block — This block is a note stating that compliance with the Commercial and Multi-Family Recycling Ordinance is mandatory.\n\nCompliance with the Commercial and Multi-Family Recycling Ordinance is mandatory for multi-family complexes,\nbusinesses and office buildings.\n\n## Block 12: text_block — This block is a note indicating where the Integrated Pest Management Plan can be found in the Official Public Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\nFor Integrated Pest Management Plan, see agreement filed in document No.____________, Official Public Records,\nTravis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 13: text_block — This block is a note stating that the project is located within the West Bouldin Creek Watershed and that no portion of the tract is within the 100-year flood plain.\n\nThis project is located within the West Bouldin Creek Watershed (Urban). All storm flows from this site will be directed\nto the West Bouldin Creek Watershed. No portion of this tract is within the boundaries of the 100 year flood plain of\nany waterway that is within the limits of study of the Federal Flood Insurance Administration FIRM panel\n#48453C0585H, dated September 26, 2008 for Travis County.\n\n## Block 14: text_block — This block is a note stating that the project is not located within the Edwards Aquifer Recharge Zone or the Edwards Aquifer Contributing Zone.\n\nEdwards Aquifer Note This project is not located within the Edwards Aquifer Recharge Zone or the Edwards Aquifer\nContributing Zone.\n\n## Block 15: text_block — This block contains the scale, mapsco number, and city of Austin grid number for the project location map.\n\nProject Location Map\nScale: 1\" = 400'\nMapsco #614S\nCity of Austin Grid #G19\n\n## Block 16: text_block — This block contains a table for recording the dates that various City departments reviewed the plans.\n\nReviewed By:\n\nDevelopment Services Department Date\nSP-2025-0126C\nDevelopment Permit No.\n\nCity of Austin Fire Department Date\n\nAustin Water Date\n\n## Block 17: text_block — This block is a note indicating that the contractor is responsible for filing all necessary forms with the Environmental Protection Agency for projects involving 5 acres or more of disturbed area.\n\nContractor is responsible for filing all necessary forms with the Environmental Protection Agency for all projects\ninvolving 5 acres or more of disturbed area or part of a larger development which will eventually disturb 5 acres or more.\nNote The contractor and the owner both must file a Notice of Intent.\n\n## Block 18: text_block — This block is a note requiring all potable water system components installed after January 4, 2014, to be \"lead free\" according to the US Safe Drinking Water Act. It lists examples of exempt and non-exempt components.\n\nAll potable water system components installed after January 4, 2014, shall be essentially \"lead free\" according to the\nUS Safe Drinking Water Act. Examples are valves (corporation stop, curb stop, and pressure reducing), nipples,\nbushings, pipe, fittings and backflow preventers. Fire hydrants, tapping saddles and 2 inch and larger gate valves are\nthe only components exempt from this requirement. Components that are not clearly identified by the manufacturer as\nmeeting this requirement either by markings on the component or on the packaging shall not be installed.\n\n## Block 19: text_block — This block is a note regarding the project's private hydrants and the owner's responsibility to comply with the Austin Fire Code. It states that private hydrants must be serviced, maintained, and flowed annually.\n\nCity of Austin - Water and Wastewater Utility - Special Services Division (512) 972-1060\nThis project has private hydrants located within the property. The property owner is required to comply with Austin\nFire Code. Failure to comply may result in civil and/or criminal remedies available to the City. The performance of\nthis obligation shall always rest with the owner or record. Fire hydrants on private property are required to serviced,\nmaintained and flowed annually, using a contractor registered with the City to provide this service. This project\nincludes __1__ private hydrants.\n\n## Block 20: text_block — This block states that a civil engineer registered in Texas must certify the plan as complete, accurate, and in compliance with the Land Development Code, unless waived by the director.\n\nA civil engineer registered in Texas must certify a plan or plat as complete, accurate, and in compliance with Chapter\n25-8 Subchapter A of the Land Development Code. The director may waive this requirement after making a\ndetermination that the plan or plat includes only minor alterations or improvement that do not require the services of an\nengineer.\n\n## Block 21: text_block — This block is a note regarding the placement of silt fences and additional erosion/sedimentation controls as required by the City's Environmental and Conservation Services Department. It also specifies that only approved construction entrances should be used.\n\nAll temporary spoils and equipment storage areas shall have silt fence placed along the perimeter of the downslope\nside. Additional erosion/sedimentation controls may be required at the direction of the City's Environmental and\nConservation Services Department Officer/Inspector. The contractor shall work closely with all City Personnel to insure\nadequacy of placement and maintenance of all erosion/sedimentation control devices. Only those construction\nentrances shown on the approved site plan shall be used for ingress and egress to and from site. In the event that\nadditional entrance locations are required to facilitate placement of materials, the contractor shall construct a stabilized\nconstruction entrance in accordance with City of Austin Standard Details in order to comply with all provisions of the\nCity's Land Development Code and Environmental Protection Ordinances.\n\n## Block 22: text_block — This block is a note stating that approval by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only and that approval by other governmental entities may be required.\n\nApproval of these plans by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only. Approval by\nother governmental entities may be required prior to the start of construction. The applicant is responsible for\ndetermining what additional approvals may be necessary.\n\n## Block 23: text_block — This block is a note stating that the project received approval from the Watershed Protection Department for a waiver to a specific section of the COA Drainage Criteria Manual.\n\nDCM Waiver Note: On 01/21/2026 this project received approval from the Watershed Protection Department of a\nwaiver to Section 1.2.4.E.1.e.i of the COA Drainage Criteria Manual.\n\n## Block 24: text_block — This block contains a stamp for the City of Austin to officially approve the site plan. It includes fields for the file number, application date, approval date, whether it was approved administratively or by commission, the expiration date, case manager, and zoning.\n\nSITE PLAN APPROVAL SHEET ____1____ of ____39____\nFILE NUMBER ____SP-2025-0126C____ APPLICATION DATE ____05/20/2025____\nAPPROVED ON _________________ \n□ADMINISTRATIVELY \n□BY COMMISSION\nUNDER SECTION 112 OF CHAPTER 25-5 OF THE CITY OF AUSTIN CODE.\nEXPIRATION DATE (25-5-81,LDC) ___________________ CASE MANAGER ___Jim Gobel_______\n____________________________________________________________________________________________\nAustin Development Services\nRELEASED FOR GENERAL COMPLIANCE _________________ ZONING ____MF-2____\n____________________________________________________________________________________________\nApproval Stamps:\n\n## Block 25: text_block — This block contains a certification statement signed by the engineer, stating that the engineering work complies with the Texas Engineering Practice Act and the City of Austin's Land Development Code.\n\nI Jason K. Rodgers do hereby certify that the engineering work being submitted herein complies with all provisions of\nthe Texas Engineering Practice Act, including Section 131.152(e). I hereby acknowledge that any misrepresentation\nregarding this certification constitutes a violation of the Act, and may result in criminal, civil and/or administrative\npenalties against me, as authorized by the Act. The plan or plat is complete, accurate and in compliance with Chapter\n25-8 Subchapter A of the Land Development Code.\nI certify that these engineering documents are complete, accurate and adequate for the intended purposes,\nincluding construction, but are not authorized for construction prior to formal City approval.\n\n[Signature of Jason K. Rodgers]\nJason K. Rodgers\n2/27/26\n\n## Block 26: text_block — This block contains notes regarding the requirement for a temporary traffic control plan (TCP) to be reviewed and approved prior to construction.\n\nTraffic Control Plan Note:\nThis note is being placed on the plan set in the absence of a temporary traffic control plan (TCP) with the full\nunderstanding that an engineered TCP shall be reviewed and approved by the Right of Way Management\nDivision. Furthermore, a TCP shall be submitted to TCPReview@austintexas.gov for review a minimum of 6\nweeks prior to the start of construction. The applicant/project representative further recognizes that a TCP review\nfee is required for the initial review and all re- reviews, as prescribed by the most current version of the City's fee\nordinance.\n\nThe following must be taken into consideration:\n· Refer to the “Mobility Guidelines” for developing traffic control strategies\nhttp://www.austintexas.gov/page/mobility-guidelines.\n· TCPs shall not be approved without an approved site plan, site plan exemption, or general permit.\n· A traffic control plan is not a permit.\n\n## Block 27: text_block — This block is a note stating that the site plan is subject to Subchapter E of the Land Development Code (Commercial Design Standards) and that compliance is required during building code review.\n\nThis site plan is subject to Subchapter E of the Land Development Code (Commercial Design Standards). Compliance\nwith Building Design Standards, Article 3 of Subchapter E, is required, and is to be reviewed for compliance during\nbuilding code review.\n\n## Block 28: text_block — This block states that a fee in-lieu of parkland dedication must be paid prior to the issuance of the Certificate of Occupancy.\n\nA fee in-lieu of parkland dedication must be paid for 39 residential units prior to the issuance of the Certificate of\nOccupancy. No Certificate of Occupancy may be issued for this development until receipt of payment. Contact the\nParks and Recreation Department prior to requesting inspections.\n\n## Block 29: text_block — This block is a disclaimer stating that the City's review of the application does not verify the data, information, and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the submittal's completeness, accuracy, and adequacy.\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the\napplicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her\nsubmittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n\n## Block 30: text_block — This block is a note stating that development of structures requiring a building permit must comply with the City of Austin Street Impact Fee Ordinances and must be paid upon completion of the building permit plan review.\n\nDevelopment of structures that require a building permit within this Site Plan, or revisions thereof, are required to\ncomply with the City of Austin Street Impact Fee Ordinances, as applicable, and must be paid upon completion of the\nbuilding permit plan review for each building. Reference: LDC 25-6-662\n\n## Block 31: text_block — This block is a note regarding procedures to follow if an underground storage tank (UST) is found during construction.\n\nIf at any time during construction of this project as underground storage tank (UST) is found, construction in that area\nmust stop until a City of Austin UST Construction Permit is applied for and approved. Any UST removal work must be\nconducted by a UST Contractor that is registered with the Texas Commission on Environmental Quality (TCEQ).\nContact UST@austintexas.gov if you have any questions.[COA Title 6]\n\n## Block 32: text_block — This block is a note stating that the engineer who prepared the plans is responsible for their adequacy and that the City of Austin relies on their work.\n\nAll responsibility for the adequacy of these plans remains with the Engineer who prepared them. In reviewing these\nplans, the City of Austin must rely upon the adequacy of the work of the Design Engineer.\n\n## Block 33: text_block — This block is a note stating that the proposed stacked biofiltration/detention pond will be privately maintained.\n\nThe proposed stacked biofiltration/detention pond will\nbe privately maintained.\n\n","numLines":277,"startLine":1,"totalLines":277}}}
