{"level":30,"time":1778187596120,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PrbA13Ggk9sAwyDEkSCfy9","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 35 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 4: notes — Plant list section heading.\n     4→\n     5→PLANT LIST\n     6→\n     7→## Block 5: notes — Notes related to the plant list.\n     8→\n     9→NOTES:\n    10→1. THE CONTRACTORS (GENERAL AND SUBCONTRACTORS) SHALL PROVIDE UNIT COSTS FOR ALL\n    11→SOFTSCAPE AND HARDSCAPE MATERIAL SPECIFIED ON THE DRAWINGS AND SPECIFICATIONS. UNIT\n    12→COSTS SHALL BE PROVIDED FOR MATERIALS AND INSTALLATION SEPARATELY. UNIT COSTS SHALL\n    13→BE: 'EACH' FOR PLANT MATERIAL, 'SQUARE FOOT' OR 'SQUARE YARD' FOR PAVEMENTS, LINEAR FOOT\n    14→FOR WALL AND FENCE, 'EACH' FOR SITE FURNISHINGS AND SITE AMENITIES, 'CUBIC YARD' OR 'CUBIC\n    15→FOOT' FOR SOIL, MULCH AND OTHER BULK PRODUCTS AND 'EACH' OR 'LUMP SUM' FOR\n    16→MISCELLANEOUS ITEMS. THE UNIT COST SHALL BE FORMATTED TO HAVE COLUMNS FOR; ITEMS, UNIT,\n    17→UNIT COST, TOTAL ITEM COST.\n    18→2. PLANT QUANTITIES ARE PROVIDED FOR OWNER CONVENIENCE ONLY. CONTRACTOR IS\n    19→RESPONSIBLE FOR VERIFYING AND PROVIDING PLANT QUANTITIES SHOWN ON THE LANDSCAPE PLAN.\n    20→3. CONTRACTOR IS RESPONSIBLE FOR LOCATING AND SECURING TREE AND PLANT MATERIAL IN\n    21→ADVANCE. BECAUSE OF THE LONG LEAD TIME ON THIS INSTALLATION AND THE RECENT SHORTAGE\n    22→ON TREE CROP, CONTRACTOR SHALL RESERVE OR CONTRACT GROW TREES WITH A GROWER.\n    23→\n    24→## Block 6: notes — Notes on special construction techniques.\n    25→\n    26→SPECIAL CONSTRUCTION TECHNIQUES\n    27→ECM 3.5.4 (D)\n    28→1. PRIOR TO EXCAVATION WITHIN TREE DRIP LINES OR THE REMOVAL OF\n    29→TREES ADJACENT TO OTHER TREES THAT ARE TO REMAIN, MAKE A\n    30→CLEAN CUT BETWEEN THE DISTURBED AND UNDISTURBED ROOT ZONES\n    31→WITH A ROCK SAW OR SIMILAR EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n    32→2. IN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS THAT CANNOT BE PROTECTED DURING\n    33→CONSTRUCTION WITH FENCING AND WHERE HEAVY VEHICULAR TRAFFIC\n    34→IS ANTICIPATED, COVER THOSE AREAS WITH A MINIMUM OF 12 INCHES OF\n    35→ORGANIC MULCH TO MINIMIZE SOIL COMPACTION. IN AREAS WITH HIGH\n    36→SOIL PLASTICITY GEOTEXTILE FABRIC, PER STANDARD SPECIFICATION\n    37→620S, SHOULD BE PLACED UNDER THE MULCH TO PREVENT EXCESSIVE\n    38→MIXING OF THE SOIL AND MULCH. ADDITIONALLY, MATERIAL SUCH AS\n    39→PLYWOOD AND METAL SHEETS, COULD BE REQUIRED BY THE CITY\n    40→ARBORIST TO MINIMIZE ROOT IMPACTS FROM HEAVY EQUIPMENT.\n    41→3. ONCE THE PROJECT IS COMPLETED, ALL MATERIALS SHOULD BE\n    42→REMOVED, AND THE MULCH SHOULD BE REDUCED TO A DEPTH OF 3\n    43→INCHES. PERFORM ALL GRADING WITHIN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS BY\n    44→HAND OR WITH SMALL EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n    45→4. WATER ALL TREES MOST HEAVILY IMPACTED BY CONSTRUCTION\n    46→ACTIVITIES DEEPLY ONCE A WEEK DURING ACCUMULATION OF THE\n    47→LEAVES.\n    48→5. WHEN INSTALLING CONCRETE ADJACENT TO THE ROOT ZONE OF A TREE,\n    49→USE A PLASTIC VAPOR BARRIER BEHIND THE CONCRETE TO PROHIBIT\n    50→LEACHING OF LIME INTO THE SOIL.\n    51→\n    52→## Block 8: notes — Notes related to tree preservation calculations.\n    53→\n    54→NOTES:\n    55→1. CHINABERRY AND BRADFORD PEAR ARE INVASIVE TREES AND REQUIRE NO MITIGATION.\n    56→2. ASH TREES REQUIRE NO MITIGATION DUE TO EMERALD ASH BORER.\n    57→\n    58→## Block 11: table — Table listing shade trees.\n    59→\n    60→SHADE TREES\n    61→\n    62→| SYM. | QTY. | COMMON NAME, BOTANICAL NAME | MIN. SPEC. |\n    63→|---|---|---|---|\n    64→| [circle with dot] | | EXISTING TREE, TO BE PERSERVED | |\n    65→| [circle with cross] | 2 | LIVE OAK, QUERCUS VIRGINIANA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n    66→| [circle with hash] | 10 | CEDAR ELM, ULMUS CRASSIFOLIA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n    67→\n    68→## Block 12: table — Calculations related to landscaping.\n    69→\n    70→CITY OF AUSTIN\n    71→LANDSCAPE CALCULATIONS:\n    72→\n    73→STREET YARD AREA:\n    74→| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n    75→|---|---|---|\n    76→| TOTAL SITE AREA | N/A | 94,203 SF |\n    77→| TOTAL STREET-YARD AREA | N/A | 6,314 SF |\n    78→| STREET-YARD / LANDSCAPE (20%) | 1,263 SF | 5,206 SF |\n    79→\n    80→STREET YARD TREES:\n    81→EXISTING TREES CREDIT\n    82→| | | |\n    83→|---|---|---|\n    84→| 2\" DIAMETER TO 6\" DIAMETER | 0 | |\n    85→| 6\" DIAMETER OR GREATER | 4 | |\n    86→\n    87→EXISTING TREES CREDIT | PROVIDED |\n    88→|---|---|\n    89→| | 8 CREDITS |\n    90→\n    91→PROPOSED TREES CREDIT | PROVIDED |\n    92→|---|---|\n    93→| | 0 CREDITS |\n    94→\n    95→TOTAL STREET YARD TREES, EXISTING TREES CREDIT PLUS PROPOSED TREES CREDIT:\n    96→| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n    97→|---|---|---|\n    98→| | 5 | 8 |\n    99→\n   100→LANDSCAPE IN PARKING LOTS:\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49385103-a1b8-4868-b0ea-01fec564ebbd","uuid":"d3e88057-d773-4e1d-ae76-66dca00981d4","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-35/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 35 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 4: notes — Plant list section heading.\n\nPLANT LIST\n\n## Block 5: notes — Notes related to the plant list.\n\nNOTES:\n1. THE CONTRACTORS (GENERAL AND SUBCONTRACTORS) SHALL PROVIDE UNIT COSTS FOR ALL\nSOFTSCAPE AND HARDSCAPE MATERIAL SPECIFIED ON THE DRAWINGS AND SPECIFICATIONS. UNIT\nCOSTS SHALL BE PROVIDED FOR MATERIALS AND INSTALLATION SEPARATELY. UNIT COSTS SHALL\nBE: 'EACH' FOR PLANT MATERIAL, 'SQUARE FOOT' OR 'SQUARE YARD' FOR PAVEMENTS, LINEAR FOOT\nFOR WALL AND FENCE, 'EACH' FOR SITE FURNISHINGS AND SITE AMENITIES, 'CUBIC YARD' OR 'CUBIC\nFOOT' FOR SOIL, MULCH AND OTHER BULK PRODUCTS AND 'EACH' OR 'LUMP SUM' FOR\nMISCELLANEOUS ITEMS. THE UNIT COST SHALL BE FORMATTED TO HAVE COLUMNS FOR; ITEMS, UNIT,\nUNIT COST, TOTAL ITEM COST.\n2. PLANT QUANTITIES ARE PROVIDED FOR OWNER CONVENIENCE ONLY. CONTRACTOR IS\nRESPONSIBLE FOR VERIFYING AND PROVIDING PLANT QUANTITIES SHOWN ON THE LANDSCAPE PLAN.\n3. CONTRACTOR IS RESPONSIBLE FOR LOCATING AND SECURING TREE AND PLANT MATERIAL IN\nADVANCE. BECAUSE OF THE LONG LEAD TIME ON THIS INSTALLATION AND THE RECENT SHORTAGE\nON TREE CROP, CONTRACTOR SHALL RESERVE OR CONTRACT GROW TREES WITH A GROWER.\n\n## Block 6: notes — Notes on special construction techniques.\n\nSPECIAL CONSTRUCTION TECHNIQUES\nECM 3.5.4 (D)\n1. PRIOR TO EXCAVATION WITHIN TREE DRIP LINES OR THE REMOVAL OF\nTREES ADJACENT TO OTHER TREES THAT ARE TO REMAIN, MAKE A\nCLEAN CUT BETWEEN THE DISTURBED AND UNDISTURBED ROOT ZONES\nWITH A ROCK SAW OR SIMILAR EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n2. IN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS THAT CANNOT BE PROTECTED DURING\nCONSTRUCTION WITH FENCING AND WHERE HEAVY VEHICULAR TRAFFIC\nIS ANTICIPATED, COVER THOSE AREAS WITH A MINIMUM OF 12 INCHES OF\nORGANIC MULCH TO MINIMIZE SOIL COMPACTION. IN AREAS WITH HIGH\nSOIL PLASTICITY GEOTEXTILE FABRIC, PER STANDARD SPECIFICATION\n620S, SHOULD BE PLACED UNDER THE MULCH TO PREVENT EXCESSIVE\nMIXING OF THE SOIL AND MULCH. ADDITIONALLY, MATERIAL SUCH AS\nPLYWOOD AND METAL SHEETS, COULD BE REQUIRED BY THE CITY\nARBORIST TO MINIMIZE ROOT IMPACTS FROM HEAVY EQUIPMENT.\n3. ONCE THE PROJECT IS COMPLETED, ALL MATERIALS SHOULD BE\nREMOVED, AND THE MULCH SHOULD BE REDUCED TO A DEPTH OF 3\nINCHES. PERFORM ALL GRADING WITHIN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS BY\nHAND OR WITH SMALL EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n4. WATER ALL TREES MOST HEAVILY IMPACTED BY CONSTRUCTION\nACTIVITIES DEEPLY ONCE A WEEK DURING ACCUMULATION OF THE\nLEAVES.\n5. WHEN INSTALLING CONCRETE ADJACENT TO THE ROOT ZONE OF A TREE,\nUSE A PLASTIC VAPOR BARRIER BEHIND THE CONCRETE TO PROHIBIT\nLEACHING OF LIME INTO THE SOIL.\n\n## Block 8: notes — Notes related to tree preservation calculations.\n\nNOTES:\n1. CHINABERRY AND BRADFORD PEAR ARE INVASIVE TREES AND REQUIRE NO MITIGATION.\n2. ASH TREES REQUIRE NO MITIGATION DUE TO EMERALD ASH BORER.\n\n## Block 11: table — Table listing shade trees.\n\nSHADE TREES\n\n| SYM. | QTY. | COMMON NAME, BOTANICAL NAME | MIN. SPEC. |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [circle with dot] | | EXISTING TREE, TO BE PERSERVED | |\n| [circle with cross] | 2 | LIVE OAK, QUERCUS VIRGINIANA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n| [circle with hash] | 10 | CEDAR ELM, ULMUS CRASSIFOLIA | 100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM CONTAINER GROWN |\n\n## Block 12: table — Calculations related to landscaping.\n\nCITY OF AUSTIN\nLANDSCAPE CALCULATIONS:\n\nSTREET YARD AREA:\n| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|---|\n| TOTAL SITE AREA | N/A | 94,203 SF |\n| TOTAL STREET-YARD AREA | N/A | 6,314 SF |\n| STREET-YARD / LANDSCAPE (20%) | 1,263 SF | 5,206 SF |\n\nSTREET YARD TREES:\nEXISTING TREES CREDIT\n| | | |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2\" DIAMETER TO 6\" DIAMETER | 0 | |\n| 6\" DIAMETER OR GREATER | 4 | |\n\nEXISTING TREES CREDIT | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|\n| | 8 CREDITS |\n\nPROPOSED TREES CREDIT | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|\n| | 0 CREDITS |\n\nTOTAL STREET YARD TREES, EXISTING TREES CREDIT PLUS PROPOSED TREES CREDIT:\n| | REQUIRED | PROVIDED |\n|---|---|---|\n| | 5 | 8 |\n\nLANDSCAPE IN PARKING LOTS:","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":259}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778187596122,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UT15x94xN2BiNPf3Xgdtiq","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 32 — Tree List\n     2→\n     3→This page provides a detailed list of surveyed trees.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet contains the comprehensive Tree List for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to inventory all surveyed trees on the site, detail their current condition, specify whether they will remain or be removed, and calculate the total required tree protection and tree mitigation in inches based on local codes.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values**:\n    10→- Total Required Tree Protection: 672.00 inches\n    11→- Total Removed Trees: 306.00 inches\n    12→- Total Required Tree Mitigation: 217.00 inches\n    13→- Total Surveyed Heritage Trees (24\" & UP): 212 inches\n    14→- Total Surveyed ECM Appendix F Trees (19\" - 23.9\"): 99 inches\n    15→- Total Surveyed ECM Appendix F Trees (8\" - 18.9\"): 295 inches\n    16→- Tree Survey Date: 03/28/2025\n    17→\n    18→**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n    19→- **Block 3: table — Tree Survey and Mitigation Table**: This is the primary data source on the sheet. READ the block content for the line-item details of every tree, including its tag number, diameter in inches, species, arborist-assessed condition, and mitigation status. Read the far-right and bottom rows for the exact calculations of required tree protection inches, removal inches, and mitigation inches categorized by tree type (Heritage, Non-Heritage, Appendix F, etc.).\n    20→- **Block 10: text_block — Survey Note**: READ the block content to confirm the source of the foundational tree data (Sinclair Land Surveying, Inc.) and the date the survey was completed (03/28/2025).\n    21→- **Blocks 4, 9, 11: text_blocks — Project Information**: READ for project identity, confirming the name (\"Valley View Townhomes\"), location (3759 Valley View Road, Austin, Texas 78704), and the owner entity (WM 41 Austin RE, LLC).\n    22→- **Blocks 1, 7: various — Landscape Architect Information**: READ for the contact information and branding for the landscape architecture firm responsible for the mitigation plan, BLU FISH Collaborative. \n    23→- **Blocks 2, 5, 6, 8, 12, 13: various — Boilerplate & Administrative**: READ for sheet metadata, including the drawing title (\"TREE LIST\"), project number (010-25-01), sheet sequence (29 of 35), and the professional seal with the issue date of 02/13/2026.\n    24→\n    25→---\n    26→\n    27→- `block-3.md` — table: Table detailing surveyed trees, required protection, removed trees, and required tree mitigation.\n    28→\n    29→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    30→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49385103-a1b8-4868-b0ea-01fec564ebbd","uuid":"d7e3bf23-a589-4008-a2ca-6174679579c0","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-32/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 32 — Tree List\n\nThis page provides a detailed list of surveyed trees.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet contains the comprehensive Tree List for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to inventory all surveyed trees on the site, detail their current condition, specify whether they will remain or be removed, and calculate the total required tree protection and tree mitigation in inches based on local codes.\n\n**Key values**:\n- Total Required Tree Protection: 672.00 inches\n- Total Removed Trees: 306.00 inches\n- Total Required Tree Mitigation: 217.00 inches\n- Total Surveyed Heritage Trees (24\" & UP): 212 inches\n- Total Surveyed ECM Appendix F Trees (19\" - 23.9\"): 99 inches\n- Total Surveyed ECM Appendix F Trees (8\" - 18.9\"): 295 inches\n- Tree Survey Date: 03/28/2025\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n- **Block 3: table — Tree Survey and Mitigation Table**: This is the primary data source on the sheet. READ the block content for the line-item details of every tree, including its tag number, diameter in inches, species, arborist-assessed condition, and mitigation status. Read the far-right and bottom rows for the exact calculations of required tree protection inches, removal inches, and mitigation inches categorized by tree type (Heritage, Non-Heritage, Appendix F, etc.).\n- **Block 10: text_block — Survey Note**: READ the block content to confirm the source of the foundational tree data (Sinclair Land Surveying, Inc.) and the date the survey was completed (03/28/2025).\n- **Blocks 4, 9, 11: text_blocks — Project Information**: READ for project identity, confirming the name (\"Valley View Townhomes\"), location (3759 Valley View Road, Austin, Texas 78704), and the owner entity (WM 41 Austin RE, LLC).\n- **Blocks 1, 7: various — Landscape Architect Information**: READ for the contact information and branding for the landscape architecture firm responsible for the mitigation plan, BLU FISH Collaborative. \n- **Blocks 2, 5, 6, 8, 12, 13: various — Boilerplate & Administrative**: READ for sheet metadata, including the drawing title (\"TREE LIST\"), project number (010-25-01), sheet sequence (29 of 35), and the professional seal with the issue date of 02/13/2026.\n\n---\n\n- `block-3.md` — table: Table detailing surveyed trees, required protection, removed trees, and required tree mitigation.\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":30}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778187596357,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR23TNZQM21DP3XA19MANDR4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Key findings from the blocks review:\n\nSheet 1 (Cover):\n- Block 5 mentions: \"Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\" (Block 5 of Sheet 9, not Sheet 1 but in the release notes)\n- No specific notes about overhead electric clearances or 48-hour notification\n\nSheet 9 (Site Plan):\n- Block 5 (notes) states: \"Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\"\n- No mention of overhead electric facilities, poles, conductors, or clearances\n- Release notes indicate utility coordination is needed\n\nSheet 21 (Electrical):\n- Shows underground electric lines, transformers, pull boxes, and electrical meters\n- The plan shows UNDERGROUND electric service (2\" HDPE water branches, 6\" PVC wastewater)\n- Block 4 utility notes: \"Contractor shall verify location of existing line, point of connection and notify engineer of any conflicts/discrepancies prior to installation of improvements.\"\n- These notes don't mention overhead facilities\n\nFrom facts.md, I found:\n- Electric easements are plat-dedicated (5' electric easements ×2, 7.5' public utility easement from the Mecey Subdivision plat)\n- This indicates there IS electric infrastructure on the property\n\nHowever, so far I haven't seen any indication of OVERHEAD electric facilities. The project appears to be planning underground electrical service.\n\nLet me check more sheets:\n1. Landscape Plan (Sheets 31-37) to see if there are any trees near overhead lines\n2. Building elevation sheets to check for overhead clearances\n3. Utility Plan details\n4. Check if there are any profile sheets showing vertical clearances\n\nLet me look for building elevations and check if there are any sections/profiles showing ov