Block 5: notes — Indicates the job number associated with the survey document.\n\nJOB NUMBER: 20240953\n\n## Block 8: notes — Certification statement and signature of the Registered Professional Land Surveyor who performed the survey.\n\nTHIS SURVEY WAS PERFORMED WITHOUT THE BENEFIT OF A CURRENT\nTITLE COMMITMENT AND THERE MAY BE EASEMENTS, BUILDING LINES,\nCONDITIONS, OR RESTRICTIONS OF RECORD NOT SHOWN OR NOTED\nHEREON.\nDATE OF SURVEY: DECEMBER 27, 2024\nRYAN T. LENZ\nREGISTERED PROFESSIONAL LAND SURVEYOR NO. 7624\nSINCLAIR LAND SURVEYING, INC.\nTBPELS FIRM No. 10089000\n4150 FREIDRICH LANE, SUITE A-1\nAUSTIN, TEXAS 78744\n(512) 443-1174\n\n## Block 9: notes — A legend explaining the symbols and abbreviations used in the site plan drawing. It defines various symbols for pins, monuments, lines, and utility features.\n\nLEGEND\n\n- 1/2\" STEEL PIN FOUND UNLESS NOTED\n- 1/2\" STEEL PIN SET W/CAP MARKED S.L.S. RPLS 518\n- 1/2\" STEEL PIN FOUND W/CAP MARKED CARTER BURGESS\n- 1/2\" STEEL PIN FOUND W/CAP MARKED LIND & ASSOC.\n- MAG NAIL SET\n- 3/4\" BRASS MONUMENT FOUND\n- NAIL FOUND\n- 60D NAIL FOUND\n- CHAIN LINK FENCE\n- IRON PIPE FOUND\n- MAG NAIL FOUND\n- \"X\" FOUND IN CONCRETE\n- WATER METER\n- WATER VALVE\n- SPRINKLE FOUND\n- WOOD FENCE\n- UTILITY POLE\n- WIRE FENCE\n- LIGHT POLE\n- CRETE FENCE\n- PUBLIC UTILITY EASEMENT\n- ELECTRIC EASEMENT\n- WATER, WASTEWATER EASEMENT\n- P.U.E. D.U.E. DRAINAGE EASEMENT\n- W.W.L.E. WASTEWATER LINE\n- W.L. WATER LINE\n- O.H.U.L. OVERHEAD UTILITY LINE\n- T.L. TOWN LOT\n- C.I. CURB INLET\n- (BRG-DIST) RECORD CALL ADJOINER\n- - DECIDUOUS TREE (VARIOUS TYPES)\n- - CLEAN-OUT\n- - FIRE HYDRANT\n- - AIR CONDITIONER\n- - SIGN POST\n- - TRANSFORMER ON PAD\n- O.P.R.T.C.TX. OFFICIAL PUBLIC RECORDS OF TRAVIS COUNTY, TEXAS\n- R.P.R.T.C.TX. REAL PROPERTY RECORDS OF TRAVIS COUNTY, TEXAS\n- P.R.T.C.TX. PLAT RECORDS OF TRAVIS COUNTY, TEXAS\n- D.R.T.C.TX. DEED RECORDS OF TRAVIS COUNTY, TEXAS\n- T. TRAFFIC SIGN\n- G. GAS MARKER SIGN\n- T. APPARENT GAS TEST STATION\n\n## Block 12: table — A table listing trees found on the property, providing an ID number, size (caliper in inches), and type (species) for each tree.\n\nTREE TABLE\n| NO # | SIZ. | TYPE |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4000 | 28\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4001 | 15\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4002 | 14\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4003 | 12\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4004 | 22\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4006 | 13\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4007 | 11\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4008 | 13\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4009 | 8\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4010 | 18\" | CHINA BERRY |\n| 4011 | 9\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4012 | 12\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4013 | 11\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4014 | 9\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4015 | 8\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4016 | 10\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4017 | 10\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4019 | 10\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4022 | 24\" | PECAN |\n| 4023 | 24\" | M |\n| 4024 | 8\" | ELM |\n| 4025 | 28\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4026 | 28\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4027 | 15\" | M |\n| 4028 | 36\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4030 | 14\" | PECAN |\n| 4031 | 16\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4032 | 18\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4033 | 11\" | ELM |\n| 4034 | 20\" | CHINA BERRY |\n| 4035 | 16\" | ELM |\n| 4036 | 14\" | PECAN |\n| 4037 | 11\" | ASH |\n| 4038 | 20\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4040 | 11\" | HACKBERRY |\n| 4041 | 13\" | ELM |\n| 4042 | 34\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4043 | 15\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4044 | 14\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4045 | 20\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4046 | 17\" | LIVE OAK |\n| 4047 | 16\" | PINE |\n\n","numLines":133,"startLine":1,"totalLines":133}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778187600188,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AUASKxBL7wFkf5bNqzwECS","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 7: text_block — Notice stating the applicant's responsibility for determining ADA compliance.\n     4→\n     5→Americans with Disabilities Act\n     6→The City of Austin has reviewed this plans for compliance with City development regulations only. The applicant, property owner, and occupant of the premises are responsible for determining whether the plan complies with all other laws, regulations , and restrictions which may be applicable to the property and its use.\n     7→\n     8→## Block 11: text_block — Legal description of the property for the site plan.\n     9→\n    10→Legal Description\n    11→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    12→\n    13→## Block 12: text_block — List of 10 site plan release notes outlining requirements and conditions for the approved site plan, including building codes, relocation costs, demolition permits, and utility service providers.\n    14→\n    15→Site Plan Release Notes\n    16→1. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require a site plan amendment and approval from the Development Services Department.\n    17→2. Approval of this site plan does not include Building Code and Fire Code approval; or building, demolition, or relocation permits approval. A City demolition or relocation permit can only be issued once the historic review process is completed.\n    18→3. All signs must comply with the requirements of the Land Development Code.(Chapter 25-10)\n    19→4. The owner is responsible for all costs of relocation of, or damage to, utilities.\n    20→5. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n    21→6. A Site Development Permit must be issued prior to an application for building permit for non-consolidated or Land Use Commission approved site plans.\n    22→8. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n    23→9. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n    24→10. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n    25→\n    26→## Block 15: text_block — List of 7 requirements from the Austin Fire Department regarding pavement, hydrants, installation timing, paving standards, dumpster placement, fire lanes, and vertical clearances.\n    27→\n    28→Fire Department\n    29→1. The Austin Fire Department requires asphalt or concrete pavement prior to construction as an \"all-weather driving surface.\"\n    30→2. Hydrants must be installed with the center of the four-inch opening at least 18 inches above finished grade. The four-inch opening must face the driveway or street with three- to six-foot setbacks from the curbline(s). No obstruction is allowed within three feet of any hydrant and the four-inch opening must be totally unobstructed from the street.\n    31→3. TIMING OF INSTALLATION: When fire protection facilities are installed by the developer, such facilities shall include all surface access roads which shall be installed and made serviceable prior to and during the time of construction. Where alternative methods of protection, as approved by the Fire Chief, are provided, the above may be modified or waived.\n    32→4. All pervious/decorative paving shall be engineered and installed for 80,000 lb. live-vehicle loads. Any pervious/decorative paving within 100 feet of any building must be approved by the Fire Department.\n    33→5. Commercial dumpsters and containers with an individual capacity of 1.5 cubic yards or greater shall not be stored or placed within ten feet of openings, combustible walls, or combustible eave lines.\n    34→6. Fire lanes designated on site plan shall be registered with City of Austin Fire Marshal's office and inspected for final approval.\n    35→7. Vertical clearance required for fire apparatus is 14 feet for full width of access drive.\n    36→\n    37→## Block 18: text_block — Locations and elevations for two benchmarks used in the project.\n    38→\n    39→Benchmarks\n    40→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    41→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    42→\n    43→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    44→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    45→\n    46→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"616e92cf-d57d-4404-a5fc-6cc71991193e","uuid":"52b8f6c7-ad35-4bcc-acff-71e371d67552","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 7: text_block — Notice stating the applicant's responsibility for determining ADA compliance.\n\nAmericans with Disabilities Act\nThe City of Austin has reviewed this plans for compliance with City development regulations only. The applicant, property owner, and occupant of the premises are responsible for determining whether the plan complies with all other laws, regulations , and restrictions which may be applicable to the property and its use.\n\n## Block 11: text_block — Legal description of the property for the site plan.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 12: text_block — List of 10 site plan release notes outlining requirements and conditions for the approved site plan, including building codes, relocation costs, demolition permits, and utility service providers.\n\nSite Plan Release Notes\n1. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require a site plan amendment and approval from the Development Services Department.\n2. Approval of this site plan does not include Building Code and Fire Code approval; or building, demolition, or relocation permits approval. A City demolition or relocation permit can only be issued once the historic review process is completed.\n3. All signs must comply with the requirements of the Land Development Code.(Chapter 25-10)\n4. The owner is responsible for all costs of relocation of, or damage to, utilities.\n5. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n6. A Site Development Permit must be issued prior to an application for building permit for non-consolidated or Land Use Commission approved site plans.\n8. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n9. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n10. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n\n## Block 15: text_block — List of 7 requirements from the Austin Fire Department regarding pavement, hydrants, installation timing, paving standards, dumpster placement, fire lanes, and vertical clearances.\n\nFire Department\n1. The Austin Fire Department requires asphalt or concrete pavement prior to construction as an \"all-weather driving surface.\"\n2. Hydrants must be installed with the center of the four-inch opening at least 18 inches above finished grade. The four-inch opening must face the driveway or street with three- to six-foot setbacks from the curbline(s). No obstruction is allowed within three feet of any hydrant and the four-inch opening must be totally unobstructed from the street.\n3. TIMING OF INSTALLATION: When fire protection facilities are installed by the developer, such facilities shall include all surface access roads which shall be installed and made serviceable prior to and during the time of construction. Where alternative methods of protection, as approved by the Fire Chief, are provided, the above may be modified or waived.\n4. All pervious/decorative paving shall be engineered and installed for 80,000 lb. live-vehicle loads. Any pervious/decorative paving within 100 feet of any building must be approved by the Fire Department.\n5. Commercial dumpsters and containers with an individual capacity of 1.5 cubic yards or greater shall not be stored or placed within ten feet of openings, combustible walls, or combustible eave lines.\n6. Fire lanes designated on site plan shall be registered with City of Austin Fire Marshal's office and inspected for final approval.\n7. Vertical clearance required for fire apparatus is 14 feet for full width of access drive.\n\n## Block 18: text_block — Locations and elevations for two benchmarks used in the project.\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n","numLines":46,"startLine":1,"totalLines":46}}}
