{"level":30,"time":1778187643871,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EzcC2T3ttWkeUBtnjpFUs8","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 11 — Grading Plan\n     2→\n     3→This sheet displays the detailed grading plan for the development site.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet presents the Grading Plan for the Valley View Townhomes multi-family development located at 3759 Valley View Road, Austin, Texas. Its primary purpose is to detail existing and proposed topography, establish building finished floor elevations (FFEs), map storm drainage infrastructure, and indicate the location of retaining walls and utility easements across the site.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values**:\n    10→*   **Scale**: 1\" = 20'\n    11→*   **Benchmark 1**: Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88\n    12→*   **Benchmark 2**: Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88\n    13→*   **Bldg 1**: 3,290 sf | FFE = 687.50\n    14→*   **Bldg 2**: 4,830 sf | FFE = 685.50\n    15→*   **Bldg 3**: 4,144 sf | FFE = 685.50\n    16→*   **Bldg 4**: 4,144 sf | FFE = 685.50\n    17→*   **Bldg 5**: 3,290 sf | FFE = 683.00\n    18→*   **Bldg 6**: 3,290 sf | FFE = 683.00\n    19→*   **Bldg 7**: 4,144 sf | FFE = 685.00\n    20→*   **Bldg 8**: 4,997 sf | FFE = 685.50\n    21→*   **ADA Ramp Maximum Slope**: 8.33%\n    22→*   **Level Landing Maximum Slope**: 2%\n    23→\n    24→**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n    25→\n    26→*   **Block 2**: Main site grading drawing showing the layout of the 8 buildings, proposed/existing contours, retaining walls, storm drainage, and various utility easements. Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, specific contour line variations, spot elevations, and the exact placement of infrastructure and adjacent property boundaries.\n    27→*   **Block 7**: Plan legend detailing line types and symbols for contours, limits of construction, storm drains, curb details, and ADA compliance parameters. READ for exact symbol definitions and maximum allowable slopes.\n    28→*   **Block 3**: Benchmark specifications detailing the exact vertical control points (Mag Nail in Asphalt) for the site. READ for exact baseline elevation data.\n    29→*   **Block 5**: Curb grading note detailing conditions for catch curbs vs. spill curbs. READ for grading flow instructions. \n    30→*   **Block 6**: Tree list reference directing the user to landscape plan sheet 32. READ for sheet reference.\n    31→*   **Block 4**: Legal description of the subject property (Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision). READ to confirm property identity.\n    32→*   **Block 1**: Graphic scale bar. READ to confirm the 1\" = 20' measurement baseline.\n    33→*   **Blocks 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14**: Boilerplate & Administrative information, including the Texas 811 call warning, engineer's seal (Jason K. Rodgers), project metadata (Sheet 11 of 39, Project No. RJ3 70496), title block, and standard utility and design disclaimers. READ for project details or administrative tracking; otherwise, these can be skipped during a technical review.\n    34→\n    35→---\n    36→\n    37→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    38→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c61cd73-4ad7-4ea5-8322-a86050b264be","uuid":"92c3ce4c-5f15-44c6-a800-e63c9ceece80","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-11/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 11 — Grading Plan\n\nThis sheet displays the detailed grading plan for the development site.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet presents the Grading Plan for the Valley View Townhomes multi-family development located at 3759 Valley View Road, Austin, Texas. Its primary purpose is to detail existing and proposed topography, establish building finished floor elevations (FFEs), map storm drainage infrastructure, and indicate the location of retaining walls and utility easements across the site.\n\n**Key values**:\n*   **Scale**: 1\" = 20'\n*   **Benchmark 1**: Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88\n*   **Benchmark 2**: Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88\n*   **Bldg 1**: 3,290 sf | FFE = 687.50\n*   **Bldg 2**: 4,830 sf | FFE = 685.50\n*   **Bldg 3**: 4,144 sf | FFE = 685.50\n*   **Bldg 4**: 4,144 sf | FFE = 685.50\n*   **Bldg 5**: 3,290 sf | FFE = 683.00\n*   **Bldg 6**: 3,290 sf | FFE = 683.00\n*   **Bldg 7**: 4,144 sf | FFE = 685.00\n*   **Bldg 8**: 4,997 sf | FFE = 685.50\n*   **ADA Ramp Maximum Slope**: 8.33%\n*   **Level Landing Maximum Slope**: 2%\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n\n*   **Block 2**: Main site grading drawing showing the layout of the 8 buildings, proposed/existing contours, retaining walls, storm drainage, and various utility easements. Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, specific contour line variations, spot elevations, and the exact placement of infrastructure and adjacent property boundaries.\n*   **Block 7**: Plan legend detailing line types and symbols for contours, limits of construction, storm drains, curb details, and ADA compliance parameters. READ for exact symbol definitions and maximum allowable slopes.\n*   **Block 3**: Benchmark specifications detailing the exact vertical control points (Mag Nail in Asphalt) for the site. READ for exact baseline elevation data.\n*   **Block 5**: Curb grading note detailing conditions for catch curbs vs. spill curbs. READ for grading flow instructions. \n*   **Block 6**: Tree list reference directing the user to landscape plan sheet 32. READ for sheet reference.\n*   **Block 4**: Legal description of the subject property (Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision). READ to confirm property identity.\n*   **Block 1**: Graphic scale bar. READ to confirm the 1\" = 20' measurement baseline.\n*   **Blocks 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14**: Boilerplate & Administrative information, including the Texas 811 call warning, engineer's seal (Jason K. Rodgers), project metadata (Sheet 11 of 39, Project No. RJ3 70496), title block, and standard utility and design disclaimers. READ for project details or administrative tracking; otherwise, these can be skipped during a technical review.\n\n---\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":38,"startLine":1,"totalLines":38}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778187643873,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QZiNUQzyGHgMu7133wm2bZ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 19 — Utility Tap Plan\n     2→\n     3→Utility tap plan showing proposed water and wastewater connections.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet contains the Utility Tap Plan for the Valley View Townhomes project, detailing the layout and connection points for proposed water, wastewater, and storm drain lines across eight multi-family buildings. Its primary purpose is to define utility routing, drainage fixture unit (DFU) loads, pipe material specifications, and regulatory compliance notes for the site's wet utilities.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values:**\n    10→*   Private domestic water branches: 2\" HDPE service tubing\n    11→*   Private wastewater branches: 6\" PVC at a minimum 2.0% slope\n    12→*   Pressure reducing valve (PRV) set pressure threshold: 65 psi\n    13→*   Number of private fire hydrants on site: 1\n    14→*   Trench depth requiring geologist inspection: Greater than 5 feet\n    15→*   Drainage Fixture Units (DFUs): 4Plex = 136 DFUs, 5Plex = 170 DFUs, 6Plex = 204 DFUs\n    16→*   Benchmark #1 Elevation: 678.52' (NAVD 88)\n    17→*   Benchmark #2 Elevation: 681.08' (NAVD 88)\n    18→\n    19→**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n    20→\n    21→*   **Block 2**: The main site plan diagram showing buildings (Bldg. 1-8), utility routing (water, wastewater, storm drain), lift stations, cleanouts, and site topography. Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, pipe routing, line lengths, and specific component locations.\n    22→*   **Block 1**: Legend defining the symbols and line types used for existing and proposed utilities (W, WW, RW, E, SD) in the main diagram. Use VISION to cross-reference symbols while reviewing Block 2.\n    23→*   **Block 13**: Utility notes specifying pipe materials and slopes for private domestic water and wastewater branches. READ for exact material sizes and installation standards.\n    24→*   **Block 7**: Drainage Fixture Unit Table breaking down the total DFU loads by building number and type (e.g., 4Plex, 5Plex, 6Plex). READ for specific sanitary load calculations.\n    25→*   **Block 17**: Benchmark data providing precise elevations and descriptions of vertical control points. READ for exact elevation values used for site grading and utility slopes.\n    26→*   **Block 10**: Water tap note outlining requirements for replacing existing Asbestos Cement (AC) pipe with Ductile Iron (DI) pipe and required hazardous disposal protocols. READ for regulatory compliance regarding existing AC lines.\n    27→*   **Block 11**: Special Services Division note mandating compliance with the Austin Fire Code for the site's private fire hydrant. READ for maintenance obligations and the exact private hydrant count.\n    28→*   **Block 5**: Technical note dictating the use of factory restrained joint pipes or mechanical joint restraint devices for all water line bends, tees, and valves. READ for specific structural requirements on water mains.\n    29→*   **Block 9**: PRV Note stipulating that any structure with water pressure at or above 65 psi must install a pressure reducing valve. READ for pressure management requirements.\n    30→*   **Block 19**: Geologist inspection requirement note triggered by the Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule for deep trenching. READ for the specific trench depth threshold (5 feet).\n    31→*   **Blocks 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20**: various — Boilerplate & Administrative. Contains the project title block, engineering firm details, Texas 811 logo, legal description, disclaimers regarding utility locations, and a cross-reference to the landscape plan for trees. READ for general project administration and liability disclaimers if required.\n    32→\n    33→---\n    34→\n    35→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    36→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c61cd73-4ad7-4ea5-8322-a86050b264be","uuid":"c23db091-0fab-4d99-9840-58ab65fd3832","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-19/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 19 — Utility Tap Plan\n\nUtility tap plan showing proposed water and wastewater connections.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet contains the Utility Tap Plan for the Valley View Townhomes project, detailing the layout and connection points for proposed water, wastewater, and storm drain lines across eight multi-family buildings. Its primary purpose is to define utility routing, drainage fixture unit (DFU) loads, pipe material specifications, and regulatory compliance notes for the site's wet utilities.\n\n**Key values:**\n*   Private domestic water branches: 2\" HDPE service tubing\n*   Private wastewater branches: 6\" PVC at a minimum 2.0% slope\n*   Pressure reducing valve (PRV) set pressure threshold: 65 psi\n*   Number of private fire hydrants on site: 1\n*   Trench depth requiring geologist inspection: Greater than 5 feet\n*   Drainage Fixture Units (DFUs): 4Plex = 136 DFUs, 5Plex = 170 DFUs, 6Plex = 204 DFUs\n*   Benchmark #1 Elevation: 678.52' (NAVD 88)\n*   Benchmark #2 Elevation: 681.08' (NAVD 88)\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n\n*   **Block 2**: The main site plan diagram showing buildings (Bldg. 1-8), utility routing (water, wastewater, storm drain), lift stations, cleanouts, and site topography. Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, pipe routing, line lengths, and specific component locations.\n*   **Block 1**: Legend defining the symbols and line types used for existing and proposed utilities (W, WW, RW, E, SD) in the main diagram. Use VISION to cross-reference symbols while reviewing Block 2.\n*   **Block 13**: Utility notes specifying pipe materials and slopes for private domestic water and wastewater branches. READ for exact material sizes and installation standards.\n*   **Block 7**: Drainage Fixture Unit Table breaking down the total DFU loads by building number and type (e.g., 4Plex, 5Plex, 6Plex). READ for specific sanitary load calculations.\n*   **Block 17**: Benchmark data providing precise elevations and descriptions of vertical control points. READ for exact elevation values used for site grading and utility slopes.\n*   **Block 10**: Water tap note outlining requirements for replacing existing Asbestos Cement (AC) pipe with Ductile Iron (DI) pipe and required hazardous disposal protocols. READ for regulatory compliance regarding existing AC lines.\n*   **Block 11**: Special Services Division note mandating compliance with the Austin Fire Code for the site's private fire hydrant. READ for maintenance obligations and the exact private hydrant count.\n*   **Block 5**: Technical note dictating the use of factory restrained joint pipes or mechanical joint restraint devices for all water line bends, tees, and valves. READ for specific structural requirements on water mains.\n*   **Block 9**: PRV Note stipulating that any structure with water pressure at or above 65 psi must install a pressure reducing valve. READ for pressure management requirements.\n*   **Block 19**: Geologist inspection requirement note triggered by the Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule for deep trenching. READ for the specific trench depth threshold (5 feet).\n*   **Blocks 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20**: various — Boilerplate & Administrative. Contains the project title block, engineering firm details, Texas 811 logo, legal description, disclaimers regarding utility locations, and a cross-reference to the landscape plan for trees. READ for general project administration and liability disclaimers if required.\n\n---\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":36,"startLine":1,"totalLines":36}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778187644998,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":21,"prompt":"Examine this electrical design plan and identify: 1) Does this plan show overhead electric lines/conductors, or only underground infrastructure? 2) Are there any utility poles shown? 3) What types of electrical infrastructure are shown (transformers, pull boxes, meters, conductors)? 4) Where is the electrical infrastructure located in relation to the proposed buildings?","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided \"Preliminary Electrical Design Plan\" for \"Valley View Townhomes,\" here are the answers to your questions:\n\n**1) Does this plan show overhead electric lines/conductors, or only underground infrastructure?**\nThe *proposed* electrical infrastructure for the site is exclusively underground. This is explicitly identified by callouts pointing to the solid blue routing lines that state exactly: **\"Underground Electric Line\"**.\nThere are *existing* overhead electric lines shown running vertically along the far western (left) edge of the drawing, outside the primary property boundary. These are indicated by dashed lines and labeled exactly as **\"OHE\"**.\n\n**2) Are there any utility poles shown?**\nThe plan does not explicitly use the text label \"utility pole.\" However, along the existing overhead electric line (**\"OHE\"**) on the western boundary, there are circular symbols (some containing the letter \"E\") which are standard architectural representations for existing utility poles supporting the overhead lines.\n\n**3) What types of electrical infrastructure are shown (transformers, pull boxes, meters, conductors)?**\nThe plan explicitly identifies specific electrical infrastructure using boxed text callouts with leader lines pointing to blue symbols. The types shown and labeled exactly are:\n*   **\"Underground Electric Line\"** (the conductors/routing)\n*   **\"Electrical Transformer Pad\"**\n*   **\"Electrical Pull Box\"**\n*   **\"Electrical Meters\"**\n\n**4) Where is the electrical infrastructure located in relation to the proposed buildings?**\n*   **\"Underground Electric Line\"**: These lines form a network routing throughout the site, running entirely outside the building footprints. They navigate around the perimeter, through common areas, and between the proposed buildings (Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8).\n*   **\"Electrical Pull Box\" and \"Electrical Transformer Pad\" locations**: These are located intermittently along the underground lines. They are situated entirely outside the building footprints, typically in open yard spaces, near property boundaries, or adjacent to drive/parking areas between building structures.\n*   **\"Electrical Meters\"**: The underground lines branch off to terminate at meter banks. The callouts for **\"Electrical Meters\"** point directly to the exterior walls of every proposed building (Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8), indicating they will be mounted on the buildings themselves.","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1385,"outputTokens":492,"totalTokens":3910,"reasoningTokens":2033},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 2092056]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisually analyze the binary file provided with extreme accuracy. Pay close attention to tables, reproducing them EXACTLY as they appear, character-by-character.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not paraphrase, interpret, or \"fill in\" text. If you cannot read something clearly, state \"text unclear\" rather than guessing. Transcribe field labels and values exactly as written.\n\nIf the requirements being validated re