{"level":30,"time":1778188070341,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR2494MSNPARJCJ9A1YMJ9NH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Perfect. I have completed the site plan review for grouping 2 (Tree Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines) with findings for all 15 checklist items. \n\nLet me verify my coverage:\n1. EL-2.1 - FAIL ✓\n2. EL-2.2 - N/A ✓\n3. EL-2.3 - FAIL ✓\n4. EL-2.4 - N/A ✓\n5. EL-2.5 - N/A ✓\n6. EL-2.6 - FAIL ✓\n7. EL-2.7 - N/A ✓\n8. EL-2.8 - NOT-VERIFIABLE ✓\n9. EL-2.9 - NOT-VERIFIABLE ✓\n10. EL-2.10 - PASS ✓\n11. EL-2.11 - FAIL ✓\n12. EL-2.12 - N/A ✓\n13. EL-2.13 - N/A ✓\n14. EL-2.14 - PASS ✓\n15. EL-2.15 - FAIL ✓\n\nAll 15 items have findings. The structured output has been returned successfully.\n\nKey deficiencies identified:\n- EL-2.1: Non-utility-compatible trees (Cedar Elm, Live Oak) within 15 feet of distribution conductor\n- EL-2.3: Large trees (40+ feet) within 25 feet of distribution conductor\n- EL-2.6: Trees within 10 feet of utility poles\n- EL-2.11: Large trees not differentiated on plans\n- EL-2.15: Trees in prohibited zones without Austin Energy approval\n\nPass items:\n- EL-2.10: Plant legend/schedule properly identifies species\n\n\n- EL-2.14: Trees and utilities both shown on landscape plan\n\nCannot verify without additional information: EL-2.8 and EL-2.9 lack sufficient detail to assess tree relationships to underground equipment and root barrier compliance. Several items marked N/A due to absence of transmission lines, easements, pad-mounted equipment, or dimensioned clearances on the plans.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2c76ca2-c20f-41b4-b01b-45ceacd50378","uuid":"fc4852db-7db3-4e6a-b658-0fb73f6a5eae"}
{"level":30,"time":1778188073857,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR2494MSNPARJCJ9A1YMJ9NH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have completed the comprehensive site plan review for **Grouping 2 (Tree Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines)**.\n\n## Summary of Findings\n\n**Key Deficiencies Identified:**\n\nThis site plan proposes landscaping improvements in areas affected by existing overhead electric (OHE) distribution infrastructure. The landscape plan shows an OHE distribution line running along the southern property boundary, with utility poles marked at regular intervals. The applicant proposes planting 12 large shade trees (10 Cedar Elm and 2 Live Oak—both non-utility-compatible species with mature heights of 40-80 feet) and retaining multiple existing large trees in close proximity to this overhead infrastructure.\n\n**Critical Issues:**\n\n1. **Non-Utility-Compatible Trees in Restricted Zones (EL-2.1, EL-2.15)**: Cedar Elm and Live Oak are not utility-compatible species and are shown positioned immediately adjacent to the OHE line without documented clearance verification or Austin Energy approval.\n\n2. **Large Trees in Clearance Zone (EL-2.3)**: Multiple large trees (40+ feet mature height) including Cedar Elm, Live Oak, and Hackberry are positioned within the 25-foot lateral distance zone from the distribution conductor without clearance documentation.\n\n3. **Trees Near Utility Poles (EL-2.6)**: Trees are positioned along the same boundary where utility poles are located, within the 10-foot lateral clearance zone.\n\n4. **Lack of Large-Tree Differentiation (EL-2.11)**: The landscape plan and plant schedule do not specify mature heights for proposed trees, preventing verification of which trees must comply with which clearance zones.\n\n5. **Underground Equipment Clearances (EL-2.8, EL-2.9)**: Underground electric infrastructure (pull boxes) is shown on the electrical plan but not overlaid on the landscape plan, preventing verification of clearances from trees to underground equipment.\n\n**Passing Items:**\n- Plant schedule properly identifies all species with common and scientific names\n- Landscape plan shows both trees and overhead facilities with clear spatial relationships visible\n\n**Applicability Notes:**\n- No transmission-class facilities present (only distribution)\n- No transmission easements\n- No pad-mounted equipment identified\n\nMy structured findings have been submitted above with detailed reasoning, code citations, sheet references, and specific recommendations for remediation."}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2c76ca2-c20f-41b4-b01b-45ceacd50378","uuid":"80725bfa-fd9b-4811-8197-a7f49b61af36"}
{"level":30,"time":1778188073895,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":501726,"duration_api_ms":186035,"num_turns":17,"result":"I have completed the comprehensive site plan review for **Grouping 2 (Tree Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines)**.\n\n## Summary of Findings\n\n**Key Deficiencies Identified:**\n\nThis site plan proposes landscaping improvements in areas affected by existing overhead electric (OHE) distribution infrastructure. The landscape plan shows an OHE distribution line running along the southern property boundary, with utility poles marked at regular intervals. The applicant proposes planting 12 large shade trees (10 Cedar Elm and 2 Live Oak—both non-utility-compatible species with mature heights of 40-80 feet) and retaining multiple existing large trees in close proximity to this overhead infrastructure.\n\n**Critical Issues:**\n\n1. **Non-Utility-Compatible Trees in Restricted Zones (EL-2.1, EL-2.15)**: Cedar Elm and Live Oak are not utility-compatible species and are shown positioned immediately adjacent to the OHE line without documented clearance verification or Austin Energy approval.\n\n2. **Large Trees in Clearance Zone (EL-2.3)**: Multiple large trees (40+ feet mature height) including Cedar Elm, Live Oak, and Hackberry are positioned within the 25-foot lateral distance zone from the distribution conductor without clearance documentation.\n\n3. **Trees Near Utility Poles (EL-2.6)**: Trees are positioned along the same boundary where utility poles are located, within the 10-foot lateral clearance zone.\n\n4. **Lack of Large-Tree Differentiation (EL-2.11)**: The landscape plan and plant schedule do not specify mature heights for proposed trees, preventing verification of which trees must comply with which clearance zones.\n\n5. **Underground Equipment Clearances (EL-2.8, EL-2.9)**: Underground electric infrastructure (pull boxes) is shown on the electrical plan but not overlaid on the landscape plan, preventing verification of clearances from trees to underground equipment.\n\n**Passing Items:**\n- Plant schedule properly identifies all species with common and scientific names\n- Landscape plan shows both trees and overhead facilities with clear spatial relationships visible\n\n**Applicability Notes:**\n- No transmission-class facilities present (only distribution)\n- No transmission easements\n- No pad-mounted equipment identified\n\nMy structured findings have been submitted above with detailed reasoning, code citations, sheet references, and specific recommendations for remediation.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"f2c76ca2-c20f-41b4-b01b-45ceacd50378","total_cost_usd":0.3031755,"usage":{"input_tokens":185,"cache_creation_input_tokens":112468,"cache_read_input_tokens":667555,"output_tokens":19130,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":112468},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":185,"outputTokens":19130,"cacheReadInputTokens":667555,"cacheCreationInputTokens":112468,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3031755,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"grouping":"2","findings":[{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.1","status":"fail","agentTrace":{"observation":"Landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows 10 Cedar Elm shade trees and 2 Live Oak shade trees proposed (Sheet 35 plant schedule lists these with 100-gallon containers and 4-inch caliper minimums). Vision analysis of Sheet 31 shows these species positioned along the southern boundary where the overhead electric (OHE) line runs, with tree canopy symbols overlapping the OHE line markings at the 1\" = 20' scale. Cedar Elm and Live Oak are not typical utility-compatible species (native spreading canopy trees with poor branch structure for coexistence with overhead lines).","reasoning":"Cedar Elm and Live Oak are non-utility-compatible species. The landscape plan shows these species positioned immediately adjacent to (and with canopy overlap at) the OHE distribution line. At the plan scale, this positioning places tree trunks and canopies within the 15-foot lateral distance zone from the overhead conductor. No clearance dimensions, compliance notes, or Austin Energy approval documentation appears on any plan sheet or supplementary document. Therefore, non-utility-compatible trees are proposed within 15 lateral feet of the overhead electric distribution conductor without documented compliance or approval.","tools_used":["vision"]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary where OHE distribution line runs"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Non-utility-compatible trees (Cedar Elm, Live Oak) are positioned along the overhead electric distribution line boundary on the landscape plan without documented clearance verification or Austin Energy written approval. Provide documented Austin Energy approval authorizing these species within the restricted zone, or relocate trees to beyond 15 lateral feet from the outside conductor."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.2","status":"n/a","agentTrace":{"observation":"Site plan documents (facts.md, README.md sheet index) contain no reference to transmission-class electric facilities (≥60kV such as 69kV, 138kV, 345kV). Only distribution-class overhead electric lines are depicted on landscape plan (Sheet 31), marked as 'OHE' with no voltage designation or transmission classification. Project facts confirm typical urban development with standard utility infrastructure.","reasoning":"Applicability is triggered only when overhead transmission lines (≥60kV) are present or proposed on the site. Facts, survey, and plan documentation provide no evidence of transmission facilities, transmission easements, or transmission-class infrastructure. The overhead lines shown are distribution-class by default per grouping guidance. This requirement is inapplicable.","tools_used":[]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":[],"sheetReferences":[],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"No transmission-class electric facilities identified on site."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.3","status":"fail","agentTrace":{"observation":"Plant schedule (Sheet 35) proposes 10 Cedar Elm shade trees (mature height ~40-50 feet, per standard tree growth references) and 2 Live Oak shade trees (mature height ~50-80 feet). Existing tree survey (Sheet 32) identifies multiple large Live Oaks (20-42 inch diameter), Cedar Elms (9-17 inch), and Hackberries (9-16 inch) to be preserved, all with mature heights ≥40 feet per ECM Appendix F standards. Vision analysis of Sheet 31 shows these large trees positioned along the boundary where the OHE distribution line runs, with tree canopy symbols overlapping the line at plan scale.","reasoning":"Large trees are defined as those with mature height ≥40 feet per the grouping's electrical utility application (ECM Appendix F). Cedar Elm, Live Oak, and Hackberry all meet this large-tree threshold. The landscape plan shows these large trees positioned immediately adjacent to the OHE line with apparent canopy overlap at the 1\" = 20' scale, indicating placement within the 25-foot lateral distance zone from the distribution conductor. No clearance documentation, approval, or compliance notes appear on the plans. Therefore, large trees are positioned within 25 lateral feet of the overhead distribution conductor without demonstrated compliance or Austin Energy approval.","tools_used":["vision"]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary where OHE distribution line runs"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":32},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Large trees (Cedar Elm at 40-50 feet, Live Oak at 50-80 feet, Hackberry at 40-60 feet mature height) are positioned along the overhead electric distribution line without documented clearance verification or Austin Energy approval. Provide clearance calculations or documented Austin Energy approval for trees within 25 feet of the distribution conductor, or relocate large trees to beyond 25 lateral feet from the outside conductor."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.4","status":"n/a","agentTrace":{"observation":"No transmission facilities identified in site plan documents, facts.md, or supplementary documents. Only distribution-class overhead electric infrastructure shown on landscape plan.","reasoning":"This requirement applies only when overhead transmission lines (≥60kV) are present or proposed. No transmission facilities are identified on this site. Inapplicable.","tools_used":[]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":[],"sheetReferences":[],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"No transmission-class electric facilities identified on site."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.5","status":"n/a","agentTrace":{"observation":"Facts.md provides detailed easement inventory. Multiple drainage, wastewater, and public utility easements are documented. No transmission easement is listed or depicted on site plans (Sheet 2 - Plat, Sheet 9 - Site Plan, Sheet 31 - Landscape Plan).","reasoning":"This requirement applies only when transmission easements are present on the site. No transmission easement is identified in any site document. Inapplicable.","tools_used":[]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4","UCM § 1.14.6"],"applicableAreas":[],"sheetReferences":[],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"No transmission easement present on site."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.6","status":"fail","agentTrace":{"observation":"Vision analysis of Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan) identifies utility poles depicted as asterisk symbols along the overhead electric line running along the southern property boundary. Landscape plan also shows trees (both proposed and existing to be preserved) positioned along the same southern boundary where the OHE line runs. Tree canopy symbols overlap the OHE line markings at the plan scale, indicating proximity of tree canopies to the utility pole structures and overhead lines.","reasoning":"Utility poles are present and marked on the landscape plan. Trees (both proposed new plantings and existing trees to be preserved) are positioned along the same boundary line where poles are located. The visual overlap of tree canopy symbols with the pole and line location at 1\" = 20' scale indicates trees are positioned within the 10-foot lateral clearance zone from poles. No clearance dimensions or compliance documentation is shown on the plans. Therefore, trees are positioned within 10 lateral feet of overhead electric utility poles without documented verification or approval.","tools_used":["vision"]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary where utility poles are located"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Trees are positioned along the OHE line boundary where utility poles are located. Provide clearance documentation demonstrating all trees are at least 10 lateral feet from each pole, or relocate trees to meet this clearance requirement."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.7","status":"n/a","agentTrace":{"observation":"Landscape plan (Sheet 31) does not show any ground-mounted pad-mounted electric transformers or equipment. Electrical plan (Sheet 21) shows underground electric service infrastructure including transformers and pull boxes, but these are underground conduits and vaults, not pad-mounted surface equipment. No pad-mounted equipment is proposed or identified as existing on the site.","reasoning":"This requirement applies only when pad-mounted electric equipment is present or proposed on the site. No pad-mounted equipment is shown on any plan sheet. The electrical infrastructure is entirely underground. Inapplicable.","tools_used":[]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":[],"sheetReferences":[],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"No pad-mounted electric equipment identified on site."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.8","status":"not-verifiable","agentTrace":{"observation":"Electrical plan (Sheet 21) shows underground electric service infrastructure including pull boxes and underground conduits for the proposed project. Landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows the layout of proposed and retained trees. Both sheets are at 1\" = 20' scale, but the exact coordinates and locations of underground equipment (pull boxes, vaults) are shown on the electrical plan while tree locations are shown separately on the landscape plan without coordinate overlay or dimensional callouts indicating distances from underground equipment.","reasoning":"The requirement applies if trees are proposed within 5 lateral feet of underground electric equipment. While underground equipment (pull boxes) is present and shown on Sheet 21, their precise locations relative to specific tree locations cannot be definitively determined from the landscape plan alone without detailed overlay analysis or distance annotations. The landscape plan does not show underground equipment locations or clearance dimensions. Therefore, whether trees are actually within 5 feet of underground equipment cannot be verified from the available plans.","tools_used":[]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Locations of underground electric pull boxes and vaults"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":21},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Underground electric equipment locations from electrical plan (Sheet 21) are not shown on landscape plan (Sheet 31). Provide landscape plan with underground equipment (pull boxes, vaults) overlaid and labeled, with clearance dimensions from proposed and retained trees to all underground equipment, or add dimension callouts demonstrating trees are at least 5 lateral feet from all underground equipment."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.9","status":"not-verifiable","agentTrace":{"observation":"Electrical plan (Sheet 21) shows underground electric service infrastructure. Landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows tree locations. No root barrier details or specifications are shown on any landscape detail sheet (Sheets 36-37 contain biofiltration pond planting details and general landscape details, but do not address electrical equipment root barriers). The spatial relationship between proposed/retained trees and underground equipment cannot be determined from plans without detailed overlay analysis.","reasoning":"The requirement applies only when trees are proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground equipment. Without locations of underground equipment shown on the landscape plan and without tree locations correlated to equipment locations, it cannot be verified whether any trees are within the 20-foot zone. Additionally, no root barrier specifications are shown on any plan detail or plant schedule. Therefore, the requirement cannot be verified.","tools_used":[]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Trees within 20 feet of underground electric equipment"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":21},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"For any trees proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment, provide root barrier details on a landscape detail sheet specifying minimum 4-foot depth and 5-foot offset from equipment. Alternatively, provide landsca plan documentation showing all trees are beyond 20 feet from underground equipment."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.10","status":"pass","agentTrace":{"observation":"Landscape Calculations sheet (Sheet 35) includes comprehensive plant schedules for all proposed planting. Shade Trees schedule lists: Live Oak (Quercus virginiana), Cedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia); Ornamental Trees schedule lists: Texas Mountain Laurel (Sophora secundiflora), Mexican Redbud (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana); Shrubs schedule lists: Dwarf Palmetto (Sabal minor), Big Muhly (Muhlenbergia lindheimeri), Gulf Muhly (Muhlenbergia capillaris 'Regal Mist'), Texas Sage (Leucophyllum frutescens 'Green Cloud'), Dwarf Wax Myrtle (Myrica cerifera 'Don's Dwarf'). All entries include both common and scientific names.","reasoning":"Plant schedule is complete and all species are identified with both common and scientific names. Requirement is satisfied.","tools_used":[]},"codeCitations":["Reviewer Convention"],"applicableAreas":["All landscape plant locations"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":""},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.11","status":"fail","agentTrace":{"observation":"Landscape plan (Sheet 31) uses symbols to denote tree types (SY = Street Yard Tree, PL = Parking Lot Tree, P = Parking Buffer Point Tree, M = Mitigation Tree) but does not indicate which trees are large (40+ feet mature height). Plant schedule (Sheet 35) lists proposed shade trees: 10 Cedar Elm and 2 Live Oak, with container/caliper specifications (100 gal., 4\" cal.) but does NOT list mature heights. Existing trees to be preserved (Sheet 32 tree list) shows trunk diameters but landscape plan (Sheet 31) does not reference the tree list or differentiate large vs. small existing trees. Therefore, on the landscape plan near overhead electric facilities, there is no visual or textual differentiation between large trees (40+ feet) and non-large trees.","reasoning":"The requirement is to differentiate large trees on the plan to allow verification of electrical clearances. Large trees (mature height ≥40 feet per electrical standards) must be distinguished from non-large trees so that clearance compliance can be assessed. The landscape plan uses hatch patterns and symbols for landscape area types and tree mitigation status, but does not differentiate tree size. The plant schedule does not include mature height specifications. Without this differentiation on the plan or schedule, a reviewer cannot determine which trees must comply with the 25-foot clearance requirement vs. the 15-25 foot requirement vs. the greater-than-25-foot zone. This deficiency prevents clearance verification.","tools_used":[]},"codeCitations":["Reviewer Convention"],"applicableAreas":["Landscape plan where trees are proposed near overhead electric facilities"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Large trees (mature height ≥40 feet) are not differentiated on the landscape plan. Add a note to the plant schedule specifying mature height for each proposed tree species, and use a distinct symbol or notation on the landscape plan to identify large trees (Cedar Elm ~40-50 ft, Live Oak ~50-80 ft, Hackberry ~40-60 ft) to allow verification of electrical clearance compliance."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.12","status":"n/a","agentTrace":{"observation":"Landscape plan (Sheet 31) contains no dimension lines, distance callouts, or measurements between trees and overhead electric facilities. The only spatial information available is the graphical layout at 1\" = 20' scale, which does not constitute a dimensioned clearance measurement.","reasoning":"This requirement applies only when tree clearances are dimensioned on plans. No clearance dimensions are provided. Therefore, this specific measurement error (using pole centerline instead of conductor) is not present on the plans because no clearances are dimensioned at all. Not applicable.","tools_used":[]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":[],"sheetReferences":[],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"No clearance dimensions shown on plans."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.13","status":"n/a","agentTrace":{"observation":"Landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows no dimension lines or distance callouts between trees and overhead facilities. Tree locations are indicated by canopy symbols positioned on the plan, but no measurements from canopy edge or trunk to conductors are provided.","reasoning":"This requirement applies only when tree clearances are dimensioned on plans. Since no clearance dimensions are dimensioned, this specific measurement error is not applicable.","tools_used":[]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":[],"sheetReferences":[],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"No clearance dimensions shown on plans."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.14","status":"pass","agentTrace":{"observation":"Landscape plan (Sheet 31) clearly depicts overhead electric lines with 'OHE -' label markings running along the southern property boundary. The plan also shows tree symbols (circles representing canopy spread) for both proposed new trees and existing trees to be preserved. Utility poles are marked with asterisk symbols along the OHE line. The spatial relationship between trees and the OHE line is clearly visible on the single plan sheet, allowing visual assessment of clearance zones and proximity relationships.","reasoning":"Trees and overhead electric facilities are both shown on the landscape plan (Sheet 31) with clear spatial relationships visible. The plan provides sufficient information to visually assess whether trees are within clearance zones (15 feet, 25 feet, etc.) relative to the depicted OHE line and poles. No separate plans or confusing cross-references prevent this verification. Requirement is satisfied.","tools_used":["vision"]},"codeCitations":["Reviewer Convention"],"applicableAreas":["Landscape plan showing overhead electric infrastructure and trees"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":""},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.15","status":"fail","agentTrace":{"observation":"Landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows trees positioned along the southern boundary where overhead electric lines run. Vision analysis indicates tree canopy symbols overlap the OHE line markings at plan scale, suggesting placement within restricted clearance zones (within 25 feet, potentially within 15 feet or 10 feet of poles). No plan notes, no approval letters, and no supporting documentation visible on any sheet or in supplementary documents reference Austin Energy approval or authorization for trees within these restricted zones. Sheet 9 (Site Plan) contains a general note 'd. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date' but no note regarding Austin Energy approval for tree placement.","reasoning":"Trees that are proposed within prohibited clearance zones (< 15 feet for non-utility-compatible trees, < 25 feet for large trees, < 10 feet from poles) require explicit written approval from Austin Energy per the grouping's 'Exceptions' section. The landscape plan shows trees within these apparent restricted zones. No documented Austin Energy approval is present in the site plan set or supplementary documents. The application includes engineer's summary letter, master comment report, completeness check results, and other documents, but none contain Austin Energy written approval for electrical clearance variations. Therefore, trees are proposed within prohibited zones without documented Austin Energy approval.","tools_used":["vision"]},"codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary where trees are positioned near OHE line and poles"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[{"documentId":"ca29fbe6-6361-4580-848e-7807ae462499","label":"Master Comment Report"},{"documentId":"726dac64-638d-4735-8a24-c3e2d243a2b8","label":"Engineer's Summary Letter"}],"comment":"Trees are positioned within prohibited electrical clearance zones without documented written approval from Austin Energy. Obtain and submit explicit written approval from Austin Energy authorizing the proposed tree placement (species, locations, and sizes) within restricted zones, or relocate all trees to comply with minimum clearance distances (15 feet from distribution conductors for non-utility-compatible trees, 25 feet for large trees, 10 feet from utility poles)."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"295c1226-f9d3-46ba-a5c4-53a7126489ad"}
