  {id: \"1:EL-1.42\", ...},\n    {id: \"1:EL-1.45\", ...},\n    {id: \"1:EL-1.46\", ...},\n    {id: \"1:EL-1.5\", ...},\n    {id: \"1:EL-1.9\", ...},\n    {id: \"1:EL-1.22\", ...},\n    {id: \"1:EL-1.27\", ...},\n    {id: \"1:EL-1.28\", ...},\n    {id: \"1:EL-1.30\", ...},\n    {id: \"1:EL-1.39\", ...},\n    {id: \"1:EL-1.43\", ...}\n]\n\nThe input contains a substantial collection of findings, with IDs spanning across the EL-1 range. There are gaps in the sequence, indicating that not all numbered findings are present in this dataset.\n\nLooking at the complete list, I can identify 30 distinct findings. The goal is to consolidate these into approximately 7-8 comments by grouping related issues. The first consolidation group should focus on elevation documentation and clearance specifications, starting with items addressing sky-to-ground clearance dimensions and related overhead conductor requirements.\n\nThe second group addresses vehicle clearances, specifically for driveways and parking areas where vertical clearance standards of 16-18 feet apply. These findings share a common requirement for documenting clearance measurements in specific contexts.\n\nFor property perimeters, there are distinct clearance requirements depending on the element type—fences need 7.5 feet from the conductor, fence posts require 10 feet from poles, and sidewalks present their own conflict considerations.\n\nRetaining walls require section views with annotated dimensions to demonstrate adequate clearance compliance.\n\nLandscape and decorative elements like trees and lighting fixtures must maintain clearances from overhead lines, with tree species selected for utility compatibility. Electrical equipment such as meters and transformers have specific working clearance and access requirements. Utility coordination involves positioning poles relative to driveways, protecting electric easements from structures, and managing water meter placements.\n\nDown guy wire anchors need to avoid accessible paths and require proper easement documentation. During construction, sites must maintain 10-foot clearance zones and provide 48-hour advance notification before work begins.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether Comment 7 should be split into separate categories. The utility coordination findings are numerous enough that breaking them into infrastructure clearances, easement protection, and utility line routing might provide better organization and clarity.\n\nHowever, looking at the actual failure statuses reveals a different priority structure. There are seven items marked as failures—1:EL-1.7, 1:EL-1.8, 1:EL-1.23, 1:EL-1.31, 1:EL-1.37, 1:EL-1.45, and 1:EL-1.46—while the remaining twenty-three are not-verifiable. This distinction suggests I should reorganize around severity levels rather than topic areas, grouping the critical failures separately from the items that need verification.\n\nThe failures cluster into distinct categories: building elevation and surveyor documentation (1:EL-1.1 through 1:EL-1.8, 1:EL-1.23), driveway clearances (1:EL-1.18, 1:EL-1.31), tree species requirements (1:EL-1.37), and construction safety measures (1:EL-1.45, 1:EL-1.46). Below these, medium-priority items cover various clearance verifications, while utility coordination issues form the lowest tier. Consolidating into roughly eight comments would allow me to address each severity band without excessive fragmentation. 30 findings total across 8 comment categories achieves the target ratio. I could consolidate some items—like merging the isolated elevated structure clearance finding with the driveway group—but the current organization keeps related issues distinct and manageable.\n\nFor the first comment, I need surveyed building elevations that document overhead conductor positions and required clearances. The drawings should show registered surveyor data with dimension lines measured from the conductor itself, not the pole, to verify the 7.5-foot vertical and 15-foot radial separation distances mandated by code. elevation drawings must show all building projections (overhangs, awnings, balconies, decks, roofs, patios) with clearance dimensions demonstrating code compliance. Structures near the Valley View Road property boundary require labeled clearance dimensions from adjacent overhead electric facilities. All elevation sheets need preparation by a registered surveyor and sealing by both surveyor and engineer, with surveyed conductor elevations and clearance annotations. Section and plan view exhibits are also needed to complete the documentation package. the most common sheets across findings. Sheets 9 and 4 appear consistently, with sheet 21 also showing up frequently across multiple findings like 1:EL-1.2, 1:EL-1.7, and 1:EL-1.8. I should focus on these high-frequency sheets rather than trying to capture every variation. Looking at the run data, I notice each finding has multiple entries with different sheet assignments and confidence levels, so I need to consolidate these into a structured format that reflects the most reliable and widely-applicable instances. nv\" status. All three runs require building elevation sheets with surveyed overhead conductor elevations and clearance dimensions to verify compliance with the 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance requirement under UCM § 1.10.3. The flagged items reference sheets 4, 9, 21, and 31, covering all proposed buildings 1-8 and their relationship to the existing overhead electric line at the southern boundary.\n\nI should map these findings to the appropriate canonical zones. Building-related items belong under \"Building Envelope,\" while utility and infrastructure concerns—including the overhead conductor clearances—fall under \"Utilities & Infrastructure.\" Site perimeter items along Valley View Road would use \"[Street Name] Frontage,\" and site-wide electrical compliance issues should be categorized as \"Utilities & Infrastructure.\"\n\nFor the specific findings: driveway clearances and retaining wall clearances likely belong in \"Site Interior\" since they're internal to the property, while fence and perimeter items could be \"[Street Name] Frontage\" if they're along Valley View Road. The OHE line appears to run along the southern boundary near Valley View Road, which helps clarify the geographic context for these clearance requirements. Valley View Road area, but the buildings themselves fall within the building envelope. For utility-related findings, I should use \"Utilities & Infrastructure,\" and for site features like landscaping or interior elements, \"Site Interior\" makes sense.\n\nLet me organize the findings by their primary focus: building-related items go under \"Building Envelope,\" utility coordination under \"Utilities & Infrastructure,\" and site-specific features under \"Site Interior.\" Now I'll structure the consolidated comments with clear headlines and detailed summaries for each finding.\n\nThe first major issue involves missing surveyed building elevations and conductor clearance documentation. I need to specify that registered surveyor data showing overhead conductor locations and clearance dimensions are essential to verify code-required clearances—both the 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance and the 15-foot radial clearance from conductors to buildings and any projections. The dimension lines must originate from the outside conductor, not the pole center. This applies across sheets 4, 9, 21, and 31, with severity and confidence both at level 3. Structural and architectural engineers need to coordinate with the registered surveyor and utility company, and the surveyor must have access to existing pole locations and conductor elevation data from the utility. I need to complete the partial thought first. The current rewritten thinking ends mid-sentence at \"Set\", so I'll finish that thought using the next thinking:\n\nSet sheets, area, and severity appropriately for the consolidated comment.\n\nNow I can see the full scope. I'm working through a structured JSON format where each comment needs to consolidate multiple test runs while preserving their individual results. The key is establishing a consistent pattern across all eight comment groups—mapping run comments into arrays, calculating confidence scores based on pass/fail distributions, and ensuring citations are deduplicated without losing any source references. I'll proceed methodically through each group to maintain accuracy and avoid redundancy in the final output.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc4d35b1-896c-4182-be6b-ea8d895e76fd","uuid":"f6308686-7fcd-4d37-8090-eeb1a00ddbc7"}
{"level":30,"time":1778188142828,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"1.json","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR249H3K9CYJRQT6TB5TJ5JW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll synthesize this grouping of electrical clearance and documentation findings into a consolidated set of review comments. Let me work through the consolidation systematically.\n\n## Analysis\n\n**Discipline:** Electrical (overhead distribution lines and clearance requirements)\n\n**Total findings:** 30 items (1:EL-1.x across multiple runs, 3 runs total)\n\n**Consolidation strategy:** Group by shared regulatory requirement and infrastructure element:\n1. Building elevation documentation (7 findings)\n2. Driveway vertical clearances (3 findings)\n3. Fence and perimeter clearances (3 findings)\n4. Retaining wall clearances (2 findings)\n5. Landscape and decorative features (2 findings)\n6. Electrical equipment access (2 findings)\n7. Down guy wire safety and easements (2 findings)\n8. Miscellaneous utility coordination (7 findings)\n9. Construction safety (2 findings)\n\n**Target consolidation:** 8–9 comments (4:1 ratio for ~30 findings)\n\n---"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc4d35b1-896c-4182-be6b-ea8d895e76fd","uuid":"1b6922d1-aedd-414f-b10f-c77646573ecd"}
{"level":30,"time":1778188174524,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"2.json","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR249S03CKJKY1MYJ6K1SF43","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, now I have the project context. This is Valley View Townhomes (SP-2025-0126C) located at 3759 Valley View Road, Austin TX 78704. It's a multi-family townhome development in MF-2 zoning.\n\nThe findings all relate to the electrical discipline (EL = Electrical) and specifically to tree clearances from overhead electric (OHE) lines and distribution conductors, as well as underground equipment clearances. This is clearly a Utility (Electrical) discipline review.\n\nNow let me analyze the 14 findings:\n\n**Finding 1: 2:EL-2.1** — Non-utility-compatible trees (Cedar Elm, Live Oak) within 15-foot restricted zone adjacent to OHE distribution lines. No Austin Energy approval documented. (3 runs, all \"f\")\n\n**Finding 2: 2:EL-2.3** — Large trees (Live Oak, Cedar Elm, 40+ feet mature height) shown directly on OHE distribution line, violating 25-foot minimum clearance. (3 runs, all \"f\")\n\n**Finding 3: 2:EL-2.6** — Utility pole locations not dimensioned on landscape plan relative to proposed trees. Clearance distances not documented. (1 run \"nv\", 1 run \"nv\", 1 run \"f\") — Note: Run 3 changed status to \"f\"\n\n**Finding 4: 2:EL-2.8** — Distances between proposed trees and underground electrical equipment not documented. Underground equipment locations not shown on landscape plan. (3 runs, all \"nv\")\n\n**Finding 5: 2:EL-2.9** — Root barrier specifications not documented for trees within 20 feet of underground electrical equipment. Trees within 20 feet not identified on landscape plan. (3 runs, all \"nv\")\n\n**Finding 6: 2:EL-2.11** — Large trees (40+ feet mature height per ECM Appendix F) not differentiated on landscape plan. \n\nThe plant schedule fails to identify them as large trees. (3 runs, all \"f\")\n\n**Finding 7: 2:EL-2.15** — Six trees positioned directly on OHE line without documented Austin Energy approval. No clearance documented. (3 runs, all \"f\")\n\n**Finding 8: 2:EL-2.12** — No clearance dimensions provided on plans. Measurement methodology cannot be verified. (2 runs, both \"nv\")\n\n**Finding 9: 2:EL-2.13** — No clearance dimensions provided. Measurement reference point (trunk vs. canopy) cannot be verified. (2 runs, both \"nv\")\n\n**Finding 10: 2:EL-2.2** — OHE voltage classification missing, so I can't determine if it's transmission (≥60kV) or distribution (<60kV), which affects whether the 50-foot transmission clearance requirement applies. (1 run \"nv\") **Finding 11: 2:EL-2.4** — Voltage classification unspecified, preventing verification of transmission clearance compliance. (1 run \"nv\") **Finding 12: 2:EL-2.7** — Pad-mounted equipment lacks dimensional reference to proposed tree locations. (1 run \"nv\") **Finding 13: 2:EL-2.10** — Trees near electric facilities aren't identified by species using a keyed legend, requiring manual cross-referencing. (1 run \"f\") **Finding 14: 2:EL-2.14** — Trees shown directly on OHE line with zero clearance.\n\nTrees positioned directly on the distribution conductor line have no documented or dimensioned clearances. (1 run \"f\")\n\nSeveral consolidation patterns emerge from these findings. Findings EL-2.1 and EL-2.15 both involve non-utility-compatible trees in prohibited zones without approval. Findings EL-2.3 and EL-2.14 both address large trees on OHE lines violating the 25-foot clearance standard—these could merge with EL-2.1 and EL-2.15 since they're all violations of the same requirement. Clearance from utility poles (EL-2.6) stands separately as a distinct issue. Underground equipment clearance problems appear in EL-2.8 and EL-2.7 with undocumented distances, and EL-2.9 regarding root barrier documentation for trees within 20 feet could fold into that group. Tree species identification and large tree differentiation (EL-2.11, EL-2.10) both concern labeling on plans and should merge. Missing clearance dimensions and measurement methodology (EL-2.12, EL-2.13) both prevent verification of distances and should combine. Voltage classification gaps (EL-2.2, EL-2.4) should merge. This yields roughly 7-8 consolidated comments from 14 findings—approximately a 1.75:1 ratio, which may not be aggressive enough for the consolidation target.\n\nLooking deeper, EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.15, and EL-2.14 all involve trees violating clearance zones from overhead electric conductors and poles, potentially collapsing into a single comment about trees in prohibited electrical clearance zones without documented approval. The distinctions matter though: non-UC trees in the 15-foot zone versus large trees within 25 feet versus trees directly on OHE lines with or without zero clearance. Most cite UCM § 1.10.10.4 as the utility clearance code, though some reference Reviewer Convention instead, which creates a different basis for the findings.\n\nFor severity, clearance violations like EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.6 run 3, EL-2.15, and EL-2.14 marked as \"fail\" represent high severity (3) due to electrical hazard potential. Documentation gaps—missing dimensions, unverified compliance, unidentified elements—marked as \"nv\" (not-verifiable) fall into medium severity (2) since they prevent verification. Administrative or labeling issues like EL-2.11 and EL-2.10 may warrant lower severity consideration.\n\nCounting across the three runs: EL-2.1 appears in all 3 runs as fail; EL-2.3 appears in all 3 runs as fail; EL-2.6 shows 2 not-verifiable and 1 fail across runs; EL-2.8 appears in all 3 runs as not-verifiable; EL-2.9 appears in all 3 runs as not-verifiable; EL-2.11 appears in all 3 runs as fail; EL-2.15 appears in all 3 runs as fail; EL-2.12 and EL-2.13 each appear in 2 runs as not-verifiable; EL-2.2, EL-2.4, and EL-2.7 each appear once as not-verifiable; EL-2.10 and EL-2.14 each appear once as fail. This gives 14 distinct findings total.\n\nThe core issue clusters around overhead electric conductor clearance violations—EL-2.1 (non-UC trees within 15 ft of distribution lines), EL-2.3 (large trees within 25 ft), EL-2.15 (trees on OHE lines without approval), and EL-2.14 (trees directly on lines with zero clearance shown)—all marked as failures across runs, representing the same fundamental positioning violation problem.\n\nUtility pole dimensioning stands separately as EL-2.6, which involves mixed flagging results. Underground equipment clearance issues appear in EL-2.8 (distances not documented) and EL-2.7 (pad-mounted equipment locations not dimensioned), both not-verifiable findings that could consolidate. Root barrier specifications for trees near underground equipment form another category with EL-2.9 appearing across all runs as not-verifiable.\n\nTree identification and labeling concerns emerge in EL-2.11 (large trees not differentiated by mature height on the landscape plan) and EL-2.10 (species not identified with keyed legend), both flagged findings tied to plan documentation standards. These administrative labeling issues carry lower severity but affect plan clarity.\n\nMissing clearance dimensions present a verification problem across EL-2.12 and EL-2.13—one lacks any dimension documentation and methodology, the other specifically questions the reference point (trunk versus canopy edge). Both prevent confirmation that clearances meet code requirements, so they consolidate into a single comment addressing the documentation gap.\n\nVoltage classification is similarly absent in EL-2.2 and EL-2.4, which is critical since transmission versus distribution lines have different clearance thresholds. Merging these into one comment about unspecified voltage classification makes sense.\n\nThe consolidation strategy groups the 14 findings into 6 comments: overhead conductor violations, utility pole clearance, underground equipment and barriers, tree identification, missing dimension documentation, and voltage specification. This achieves roughly a 2.3-to-1 compression ratio.\n\nI'm considering whether to merge the conductor and pole clearance groups since both involve tree positioning around electrical infrastructure, but they actually require different corrective actions—trees need to be 15-25 feet from conductors versus 10 feet from poles. Keeping them separate is justified.\n\nSimilarly, the underground equipment, missing dimensions, and voltage classification groups address fundamentally different problems: identifying underground infrastructure locations, documenting specific clearance measurements, and determining voltage levels. These can't reasonably combine without losing clarity on what needs to be corrected.\n\nNow I need to map out which sheets contain each issue and identify where on the property these problems occur. The overhead conductor clearances appear along the southern boundary at Valley View Road, while utility pole issues are concentrated at the southern or western boundaries. The referenced sheets cluster around 31 and 35 for most groups, with some variations.\n\nUnderground equipment conflicts exist throughout the site where utilities meet planted areas, tree identification concerns span the landscape plan zones near electrical facilities, and the missing dimension issues focus on trees adjacent to overhead lines. The voltage classification problems appear to affect the southern and western property areas. –80 feet, Hackberry at 40–60 feet mature height per ECM Appendix F) positioned within 25-foot minimum clearance zone from distribution conductors. Six mitigation trees are shown directly on the overhead electric line without documented written Austin Energy approval authorizing the clearance variance or species placement. Trees positioned directly on the OHE distribution line indicate zero clearance maintained from the conductor, with no clearance distances documented or dimensioned on the landscape plan.\n\nThe violations center on three key issues: non-utility-compatible species placed within restricted zones lacking approval documentation, mature tree heights that will exceed minimum clearance requirements, and direct placement on conductors without any clearance buffer or variance authorization. Looking at the guidance on summary and detail structure, I need to ensure the summary captures the overarching issue while details provide specific findings without redundancy. The summary should state the core violation—non-utility-compatible trees positioned in electrical clearance zones—and then each detail should add distinct findings rather than restating that same core fact. For instance, if the summary already establishes the clearance violation exists without Austin Energy approval, the details should focus on which sheets contain the violations, the specific tree types involved, or the exact distances involved, rather than repeating the approval requirement. This keeps the structure clean and prevents the reader from encountering the same information twice. ), all referencing the same code section and infrastructure issue. The core problem is that trees are positioned within prohibited clearance zones from overhead electric distribution conductors without documented approval. Each detail then specifies a distinct aspect: non-UC species in the 15-foot zone, large species with 25-60+ foot mature heights in the 25-foot zone, trees directly on the OHE line itself, and instances with zero clearance documented on the plan. This structure avoids redundancy while capturing the specific violations across different tree locations and clearance requirements. I need to complete the partial thought from the current rewritten thinking first. The sentence cuts off at \"Non-utility-compatible and large\" so I'll finish it using the next thinking:\n\nNon-utility-compatible and large trees are positioned within prohibited clearance zones from overhead electric distribution conductors, lacking documented Austin Energy approval.\n\nNow I'm working through how to structure the detailed findings for each code section. Each detail needs to stay under 30 words and avoid restating what the summary already covers—so instead of repeating \"clearance violation,\" I focus on the specific species, dimensions, and documentation gaps. For instance, EL-2.1 calls out the non-compatible species and missing approval, EL-2.3 specifies the mature heights and zone depth, EL-2.15 highlights the six mitigation trees placed directly on the line, and EL-2.14 notes the zero clearance marking without dimension documentation. I'm verifying each detail stays concise and targets the concrete deficiency rather than the general problem. Clearance distances between proposed trees and utility poles are not dimensioned on the landscape plan, preventing verification that trees meet the 10-foot minimum lateral clearance requirement from poles. Utility pole locations lack clear dimensioning relative to proposed tree positions, and documentation demonstrating at least 10 lateral feet from each pole is required, or trees must be relocated.\n\n**Sheets:** [21, 31]\n**Applicable Area:** \"Southern property boundary\"\n**Severity:** 2\n**Confidence:** 2\n**CrossDep:** \"\"\n**Attn:** \"Run 3 changed status from 'not-verifiable' to 'fail,' indicating trees may be positioned within the restricted zone upon closer review.\"\n\n---\n\n## Comment 3: Underground Equipment Clearance & Root Barrier Documentation\n\n**Headline:** \"Underground electric equipment clearances not documented\"\n\n**Summary:** Proposed tree locations lack dimensioned clearances to underground electrical infrastructure including pull boxes, vaults, and pad-mounted transformers. Root barrier specifications are missing for trees positioned within 20 feet of such equipment. (31 words)\n\n**Details:**\n- Underground electrical equipment shown on the electrical plan is not overlaid or referenced on the landscape plan, making clearance verification impossible. (21 words)\n- Pad-mounted transformer locations are not dimensioned relative to tree placements, preventing clearance confirmation from submitted documents. (16 words)\n- Trees within 20 lateral feet of underground equipment lack identified root barrier details specifying minimum 4\n\n-foot depth and 5-foot offset requirements per code standards. (25 words)\n\n**Sheets:** [21, 31, 37]\n**Applicable Area:** \"Utilities & Infrastructure\"\n**Severity:** 2\n**Confidence:** 2 for EL-2.8 & EL-2.9 (3 runs each); 1 for EL-2.7 (1 run) — overall \"2\"\n**CrossDep:** \"\"\n**Attn:** \"Overlay underground equipment locations from electrical plan (Sheet 21) onto landscape plan (Sheet 31) with dimension callouts. For any trees within 20 feet of underground equipment, provide root barrier details on landscape detail sheets per UCM § 1.10.10.4.\"\n\n---\n\n## Comment 4: Tree Species Identification & Large Tree Differentiation\n\n**Headline:** \"Tree species and sizes not differentiated on plans\"\n\n**Summary:** Large trees (mature height ≥40 feet) are not visually distinguished on the landscape plan, and tree species are not keyed by legend or labeled directly on the plan. The plant schedule lists installation heights only, not mature heights, making it impossible to identify which trees are subject to large-tree clearance restrictions. Plant schedule lacks mature height data for species like Cedar Elm (~40–50 ft), Live Oak (~50–80 ft), and Hackberry (~40–60 ft per ECM Appendix F), preventing identification of trees subject to clearance restrictions. Species identification near electric facilities requires manual cross-reference to a separate plant list rather than direct labeling on the plan.\n\n**Sheets:** [31, 35]\n**Applicable Area:** \"Landscape plan\"\n\n**Severity:** 1\n**Confidence:** 3 for EL-2.11 (all 3 runs); 1 for EL-2.10 (1 run) — overall \"2\"\n**CrossDep:** \"\"\n**Attn:** \"Tree species identification directly on landscape plan with a keyed legend will facilitate verification of electrical clearance compliance. Include mature heights in plant schedule for all tree species.\"\n\n---\n\n## Comment 5: Missing Clearance Dimension Documentation\n\n**Headline:** \"Clearance distances from trees not dimensioned\"\n\n**Summary:** No dimension lines are provided on the landscape plan showing clearances from trees to overhead electric conductors or measurement reference points (trunk center vs. canopy edge). Measurement methodology cannot be verified from available documentation. (32 words)\n\n**Details:**\n- (EL-2.12) No clearance dimensions are provided on landscape plan showing tree-to-conductor distances. Measurement methodology and measurement reference points cannot be verified from the plans. (23 words)\n- (EL-2.13) Dimension lines don't indicate whether tree clearances were measured from trunk centers or canopy edges, making it impossible to verify the measurement methodology from available documentation. (27 words)\n\n**Sheets:** [21, 31]\n**Applicable Area:** \"Landscape plan trees near overhead electric line\"\n**Severity:** 2\n**Confidence:** 2\n**CrossDep:** \"\"\n**Attn:** \"Dimension lines must be added to the landscape plan showing measured clearances from tree trunks to nearest conductor and/or pole. Specify whether measurements are taken from trunk center or canopy perimeter.\"\n\n---\n\n## Comment 6: Overhead Electric Voltage Classification\n\n**Headline:** \"Voltage classification of overhead electric lines not specified\"\n\n**Summary:** The voltage classification of existing overhead electric lines (transmission ≥60 kV vs. distribution <60 kV) is not specified on the plans. Without this information, compliance with the 50-foot minimum clearance requirement for transmission lines cannot be verified. (37 words)\n\n**Details:**\n- (EL-2.2) Voltage classification of overhead electric lines not specified on plans. Cannot determine transmission (≥60 kV) vs. distribution (<60 kV) classification needed to verify 50-foot transmission clearance compliance. (28 words)\n\n- (EL-2.4) Voltage classification not specified on plans. Cannot independently verify transmission clearance requirements are met without knowing line voltage classification.\n\nSheets 21, 31, and 35 show overhead electric lines along the southern and western property boundaries, but the voltage classification—critical for determining whether transmission or distribution standards apply—is missing. Austin Energy should be contacted to confirm the classification, then appropriate clearance distances can be verified based on whether the lines are transmission-class (≥60 kV) or distribution-class (<60 kV). All 14 elements are distributed across 6 comments with a consolidation ratio of 2.33:1. Now I need to map this to the StructuredOutput schema, which requires organizing comments with headlines, summaries, sheets, applicable areas, severity and confidence levels, cross-departmental notes, and attention flags. Each detail needs citations, source IDs, and confidence ratings, plus runComments that capture the run number, status (fail or nv), and the verbatim comment text from the input.\n\nFor EL-2.1, the three runs flag non-utility-compatible trees planted in restricted zones near overhead electric lines without proper clearance or Austin Energy approval. Run 1 notes six mitigation trees on the landscape plan with no clearance from distribution conductors and no documented UC status. Run 2 identifies Cedar Elm and Live Oak within the 15-foot restricted zone lacking Austin Energy approval. Run 3 specifies these same species positioned along the overhead line boundary without clearance verification or written approval, requesting either documented approval or relocation beyond 15 lateral feet from the outside conductor.\n\nFor EL-2.3, Run 1 begins describing large trees like Live Oak and Cedar Elm with mature heights of 40+ feet shown directly on the distribution line, violating the 25-foot minimum clearance requirement. Run 2 notes these same large species (Cedar Elm and Live Oak at 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) proposed within 25 feet of overhead electric distribution lines without clearance verification or Austin Energy approval. Run 3 expands the list to include Hackberry (40-60 feet) and provides specific mature height ranges for each species, requesting clearance calculations, documented approval, or relocation beyond 25 lateral feet from the outside conductor.\n\nFor EL-2.6, Run 1 identifies utility poles on site but notes that clearance distances from proposed trees to poles are not dimensioned or documented\n\n. Run 2 indicates pole locations lack clear dimensioning relative to tree positions, preventing clearance verification from available documentation. Run 3 specifies trees are positioned along the OHE line boundary where poles exist and requests clearance documentation showing all trees at least 10 lateral feet from each pole, or relocation to meet this requirement.\n\nEL-2.8 addresses underground electrical equipment clearances. Run 1 simply states distances between proposed trees and underground equipment are not documented. Run 2 notes specific equipment locations lack dimensioning relative to tree placements. Run 3 indicates underground equipment from the electrical plan is not shown on the landscape plan and requests the landscape plan be updated with underground equipment (pull boxes, vaults) overlaid and labeled.\n\nFor EL-2.9, root barrier specifications and depths for trees within 20 feet of underground equipment need documentation. Run 1 identifies this gap, Run 2 clarifies trees within 20 feet of equipment aren't identified on the landscape plan with corresponding root barrier details, and Run 3 requires either root barrier details specifying minimum 4-foot depth and 5-foot offset from equipment for affected trees, or documentation confirming all trees are beyond 20 feet from underground equipment.\n\nEL-2.11 concerns large tree species—Live Oak and Cedar Elm are noted as 40+ foot trees per ECM Appendix F but aren't differentiated in the plant schedule, which only documents planting size rather than mature height. The landscape plan lacks visual distinction for large trees, making it difficult to verify electrical clearance compliance. The correction requires adding mature heights to the plant schedule and using distinct symbols on the landscape plan to identify large trees (Cedar Elm ~40-50 ft, Live Oak ~50-80 ft, Hackberry ~40-60 ft).\n\nFor EL-2.15, six trees are positioned directly on overhead electric lines without documented written approval from Austin Energy author\n\n. Trees are proposed within prohibited clearance zones—non-UC Cedar Elm and Live Oak within 15 feet of overhead distribution lines—with no evidence of explicit Austin Energy authorization. The applicant must either obtain written approval from Austin Energy or modify tree species and locations to meet clearance requirements, including 15 feet from distribution conductors for non-utility-compatible trees, 25 feet for large trees, and 10 feet from utility poles.\n\nEL-2.12 identifies that no clearance dimensions are provided on the landscape plan, making it impossible to verify the measurement methodology used.\n\nEL-2.13 notes the absence of dimension lines showing whether clearances were measured from tree trunk centers or canopy edges, preventing verification of the measurement approach from available documentation.\n\nEL-2.2 points out that the voltage classification of existing overhead electric lines isn't specified, so I cannot determine whether transmission (≥60kV) or distribution (<60kV) applies and therefore cannot verify the 50-foot transmission clearance requirement.\n\nEL-2.4 similarly requires voltage classification confirmation to independently verify transmission clearance compliance.\n\nEL-2.7 indicates that pad-mounted equipment locations lack dimensioning relative to proposed trees, preventing clearance verification.\n\nEL-2.10 shows that trees near electric facilities aren't identified by species using a keyed legend, requiring manual cross-reference to determine species.\n\nEL-2.14 reveals trees positioned directly on overhead electric lines with zero clearance documented or dimensioned.\n\nNow I need to verify the citation assignments. Code violations related to clearance distances should reference UCM § 1.10.10.4, while documentation and labeling conventions use \"Reviewer Convention.\" Mapping each finding: EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.15, EL-2.6, EL-2.8, EL-2.7, EL-2.9, and EL-2.12 all cite the code section for clearance violations. EL-2.14, EL-2.11, and EL-2.10 cite the reviewer convention since they address plan documentation standards rather than code compliance. 1.10.10.4\n\nNow I need to organize the findings into structured comments. Comment 4 addresses tree species identification using Reviewer Convention standards, while Comments 5 and 6 handle missing dimensions and voltage classification respectively, all citing UCM § 1.10.10.4. With the citation mapping complete, I can now build the JSON structure, ensuring the runComments array captures every run associated with each flagged issue.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ccfa398d-4214-4f81-b393-ac32991500b5","uuid":"94c4cda9-5fad-463d-8964-48a0432e9101"}
