{"level":30,"time":1778188184096,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"13.json","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR249H2RQ57590TFSDCTQQ0W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me carefully analyze this task:\n\n1. I'm processing consolidated findings about electrical transformer pad clearance requirements for what appears to be a site plan review\n2. I need to transform 40+ atomic findings into a consolidated set of review comments\n3. The discipline is clearly **Utility (Electrical)** - focus on transformer pad placement, clearances, easements, and infrastructure coordination\n4. I need to apply the hierarchy, consolidate aggressively, and produce structured JSON output\n\nLet me start by understanding the scope and findings:\n\n**Discipline Identification:**\n- All findings relate to transformer pad placement, clearances, and electrical utility requirements\n- Primary regulations: UCM (Austin Utility Code Manual) § 1.10 (transformer clearance/access)\n- Cross-cutting concerns: building clearances, utility coordination, landscape protection, property boundaries, easements\n- Severity: Multiple failures (f) and not-verifiable (nv) findings across 3 independent review runs\n- This is purely **Utility (Electrical)** scope - Tier 1\n\n**Findings Count:** 40 distinct checklist items (13:EL-13.1 through 13:EL-13.39)\n\n**Target Output:** ~10 comments (40 findings ÷ 4 ≈ 10)\n\nLet me organize the findings by theme for merging:\n\n### THEME 1: Building & Structure Clearances (13:EL-13.1, 13.2, 13.3, 13.4, 13.5, 13.6)\n- 13:EL-13.1 - Building clearance dimensions not documented (5-foot min)\n- 13:EL-13.2 - Retaining wall clearances (5-foot)\n- 13:EL-13.3 - Fence clearances (5-foot vs 3-foot based on access)\n\n\n- 13:EL-13.4 - Reduced clearances <5 feet (3-foot to brick/masonry with AE approval)\n- 13:EL-13.5 - Balcony/overhang clearances (5-foot lateral)\n- 13:EL-13.6 - Window/door/duct clearances (12-foot for oil-filled)\n\n**MERGE DECISION:** Consolidate all six into a single comment addressing structural proximity clearances. They all reference UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 and the baseline 5-foot minimum separation requirement from adjacent structures, with specific exceptions for reduced clearances when approved by the architect-engineer.\n\n### THEME 2: Public/Site Access & Easements\n\nSite feature and equipment spacing requirements span multiple clearance standards. Sidewalk setbacks require 2-foot separation from the back of curb, while fire safety infrastructure demands 20-foot clearance for oil-filled units and 5-foot spacing at hydrants. Fire lanes need 15-foot clearance, and transformer-to-transformer spacing requires 5-foot minimum separation. Consolidate these into one comment addressing public right-of-way and on-site equipment clearances.\n\nVegetation management around the transformer pad involves selecting utility-compatible species per ECM Appendix F, maintaining 10-foot clearance on the access side and 5-foot on remaining sides, and installing root barriers at 4-foot depth positioned 5-foot from equipment. Combine these into a single comment on landscaping and vegetation control.\n\nUtility coordination with water, wastewater, and storm infrastructure requires 5-foot clearance from water lines and similar spacing from wastewater lines.\n\nUnderground utilities need 12-inch horizontal and vertical separation from non-gas utilities, with careful attention to crossing relationships and vertical separation requirements. Consolidate these into one comment addressing subsurface utility coordination.\n\nFor site access and egress, I'm grouping clearance requirements around building perimeter features: sidewalk clearances, exterior stairs if present, fire escapes, hydrants, dumpsters, bike parking, light poles, and fire lanes all need specific setbacks to function properly and meet code. The right structure groups these by infrastructure type: building and structure setbacks (13.1-13.6), pedestrian access including sidewalks and stairs (13.7-13.8), fire protection elements like hydrants (13.9, 13.12, 13.19), site features such as dumpsters and bike racks (13.16-13.18), transformer-to-transformer spacing (13.20), vegetation control (13.13-13.15), and utility coordination for water, wastewater, and storm systems (13.21-13.23).\n\nI also need to account for underground utility separation (13.25-13.26), transformer door access and hot-stick reach (13.10), confined space niche specifications (13.11), surface drainage and slope requirements (13.32), property line and easement boundaries (13.33-13.36), transformer pad construction details (13.37), bollard barriers (13.38), and overhead clearances above structures (13.31).\n\nThen there's the gas and electrical line routing (13.24, 13.27-13.28, 13.39), vault transformer specifications (13.29-13.30), and retaining wall considerations (13.2). I'm seeing significant overlap and redundancy here—I need to consolidate more strategically rather than creating a comment for each finding.\n\nThe target is roughly one comment per four findings, so 40 findings should produce about 10 consolidated comments. Let me reorganize by grouping findings that address the same core requirement: building and structure setbacks (13.1-13.6), pedestrian and site access clearances (13.7-13.9, 13.12, 13.16-13.19), equipment spacing (13.20), utility routing and clearances (13.24, 13.27-13.28, 13.39), and transformer specifications (13.29-13.30). This approach reduces redundancy while maintaining clarity around distinct compliance areas. requirements from UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4.\n\n**GROUP B: Site Feature Clearances** (9 findings)\n- 13.7, 13.8, 13.9, 13.12, 13.16, 13.17, 13.18, 13.19, 13.20\n- Covers sidewalks, stairs, fire escapes, hydrants, dumpsters, bike racks, poles, fire lanes, and inter-equipment spacing—all lower-priority items requiring clearance documentation. **GROUP C: Vegetation & Root Management** (3 findings)\n- 13.13, 13.14, 13.15\n- Governed by UCM § 1.10.10.4 and ECM Appendix F. **GROUP D: Utility Coordination - Water/Sewer/Storm**\n- 13.21, 13.22, 13.23 per UCM § 2.9.1.B.2, which mandates 5-foot clearances. **GROUP E: Underground & Gas Utilities** (3 findings)\n- 13.25, 13.24 (gas line), 13.26 under UCM § 1.10.5.A and 1.10.5.B. **GROUP F: Access Door & Hot-Stick Clearance** (1 finding)\n- 13.10 per UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4. **GROUP G: Confined Space (Niche) Requirements** (1 finding)\n- 13.11 per UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 3. **GROUP H: Vertical Clearances & Overhead** (4 findings)\n- 13.27, 13.28, 13.31, 13.39 addressing overhead conductors and vertical spacing requirements.\n\nContinuing with the remaining groupings: **GROUP I: Grading & Surface Drainage** (1 finding) - 13.32 per UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 footnote 1. **GROUP J: Property Boundary & Easement Compliance** (4 findings) - 13.33, 13.34, 13.35, 13.36 covering property location, easement relations, and electrical routing. **GROUP K: Transformer Pad Details & Design** (2 findings) - 13.37, 13.38 for pad specifications and bollard details. **GROUP L: Vault/Niche Transformer** (2 findings) - 13.29, 13.30 specific to vault configuration. This yields 11 groups across 40 findings, maintaining approximately a 3.6:1 ratio. Exploring further consolidation possibilities, particularly merging Groups F and G appears feasible. Clearances & Overhead Structures** (13.27, 13.28, 13.31, 13.39) = 4 findings\n9. **Grading & Surface Drainage** (13.32) = 1 finding\n10. **Property Boundaries & Easements** (13.33, 13.34, 13.35, 13.36) = 4 findings\n11. **Transformer Pad Details & Specifications** (13.37, 13.38) = 2 findings\n12. **Vault/Niche Transformer Installation** (13.29, 13.30) = 2 findings\n\nThat gives me 12 categories. I should consolidate further by combining access-related items (13.10, 13.11) and merging pad specifications with vault installations since both address transformer placement requirements. This reduces the groupings to 10 main categories covering all 39 findings.\n\nHowever, looking more closely at the vault and pad items—13.29 and 13.30 address vault cross-sections and fire/ventilation details, while 13.37 and 13.38 cover pad dimensions and bollard protection. These serve different purposes in the design, so keeping them separate makes more sense than forcing them together. 13.10 and 13.11 both address specific transformer pad configuration requirements—access door clearance for hot-stick operations and confined space protocols—so they fit together under a single category. Merging these brings the structure down to 11 comment groups covering 40 findings, which improves the ratio to 3.6:1 while keeping related requirements consolidated.\n\nLooking at the remaining items, 13.6 (window and door clearance at 12 feet) aligns with the broader building proximity standards, and 13.29 and 13.30 (vault niche installations) should stay separate since they only apply when that specific configuration is used. Consolidating further to exactly 10 comment groups means grouping the clearance requirements by type—building structures, site features and equipment, vegetation, utilities, and underground infrastructure—which creates a cleaner organizational structure that's easier to reference and verify during design review. Installation & Site Preparation** (13.29, 13.30, 13.37, 13.38)\n\nI'm settling on 11 consolidated comments since the spec calls for roughly one per four findings, and with 40 findings that gives me about 10—this allows for reasonable flexibility. The consolidation is aggressive enough to meet the intent while keeping related items grouped logically. My final structure groups clearances by type (building structures, site features, vegetation), then utility systems (water/wastewater/storm, underground/gas, overhead), followed by site conditions (grading, boundaries), and transformer-specific requirements (access, confined space, installation prep). The current grouping needs refinement. Merging groups 9 and 10 makes sense since both address the transformer's physical layout and access needs. Group 7 is problematic—13.32 about oil-filled versus dry-type equipment and grading requirements doesn't fit neatly anywhere. Looking at it more carefully, this requirement combines equipment type specification with drainage planning, so it belongs with equipment specifications rather than standing alone. The revised structure should be: Building & Structure Proximity Clearances, Site Features & Equipment Clearances, Vegetation & Root Management, and Utility Coordination for water and wastewater systems. 13.30, 13.32, 13.37, 13.38) - 5 findings\n\nTotal: 40 findings across 9 categories. This distribution feels right—the building proximity group is the largest, followed by site features and equipment clearances, which makes sense given how many spatial constraints exist on a typical site. The smaller categories like vegetation management and transformer access are appropriately sized for their specific focus areas. Considering whether to split transformer specifications into separate comments for pad details versus grading and drainage. The key distinction is that 13.32 addresses surface drainage flowing away from transformer pads and building structures—this is fundamentally a site design issue rather than a clearance concern, so it makes sense to keep it grouped with other pad specifications rather than merging it into building proximity requirements.\n\nOrganizing the findings into ten distinct comments: building and structure proximity clearances covering the foundational distance requirements; site features and equipment clearances addressing interactions with various site elements; vegetation and root management for landscape considerations; and utility coordination for water, wastewater, and storm systems. Pad dimensions and clearances not dimensioned. Retaining walls are present but their clearances aren't documented per UCM § 1.10.4. Fence clearances and door orientations remain unspecified—particularly for Transformer Pad 4, which sits very close to the southern property fence with no documented measurements. Reduced clearances under 5 feet to brick or masonry structures aren't shown on the plans.\n\nIf reduced clearances are proposed, architectural engineer design approval and 3-hour fire ratings would be required. Balcony and overhang clearances lack documentation, and building elevations and sections haven't been provided to show window, door, and duct clearances relative to equipment.\n\nThe transformer pad locations don't have documented horizontal and vertical clearances to adjacent buildings, structures, fences, and architectural features. The minimum 5-foot clearance isn't dimensioned on the plans, and special requirements like reduced 3-foot clearances to masonry or 12-foot clearances for windows/doors/ducts on oil-filled units aren't addressed. Building clearances need to be verified and dimensioned per UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4, and retaining walls on the site require clearance documentation as well.\n\nTransformer Pad 4 sits very close to the southern property fence without documented clearances, and the access door orientation isn't identified, so I can't determine whether the 5-foot (access side) or 3-foot (non-access) clearance requirement applies. If any transformer pads are proposed with less than 5-foot clearance to masonry, the reduced clearance provisions need to be evaluated.\n\nBuilding elevations haven't been provided, so I can't verify that balconies, overhangs, or covered structures maintain the required 5-foot lateral clearance from transformer pads. Additionally, without submitted building elevations and equipment type specifications (oil-filled versus dry-type), I cannot confirm the 12-foot minimum clearance requirements are met.\n\nThe findings consistently flagged across all three runs indicate critical safety and compliance issues related to building clearances. Architecture needs to submit elevations showing window and door locations along with equipment access paths so clearances can be properly coordinated. If any pads are proposed with reduced clearances, Austin Energy Design approval will be required before plan approval can proceed.\n\nSite features and equipment spacing present another significant concern. The findings span pedestrian areas like sidewalks and stairs, fire safety elements including fire escapes and hydrants, site amenities such as dumpsters and bike racks, plus transformer-to-transformer spacing. None of these have documented minimum distances on the plans, which prevents verification of code compliance. Sidewalk clearances specifically fall under UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 and require dimensioned documentation. pads are provided. Transformer type not specified.\" Fire hydrant locations appear on the fire protection plan but lack dimensioned clearances from transformer pads on the electrical drawings. Dumpster and trash enclosure locations, if present, aren't identified with their required 5-foot setbacks from transformers. Bicycle parking also needs dimensioning on the site plan to verify adequate separation from transformer equipment.\n\nLighting plan details are missing, so pole locations relative to transformer pads can't be verified for compliance. Transformer Pad 4 in the south-central area near the fire lane lacks documentation of the required 15-foot clearance from pad edge to fire lane centerline. Additionally, the multiple transformer pads shown don't have dimensions confirming the minimum 5-foot spacing between each pad.\n\nThe clearance issues are primarily documentation gaps rather than actual design problems, though they affect multiple areas of the site. Most findings appear consistently across reviews. I need to confirm which site features—dumpsters, bike racks, light poles, stairs—are actually included in this project, since some requirements only apply if those elements are present.\n\nFor the landscaping, the proposed trees and plantings haven't been checked against utility compatibility standards, and there's no documentation of protective clearances or root barriers relative to the transformer pads as required by code. lateral feet of transformer pads not identified. Root barrier specifications (minimum 4-foot depth, installed 5 feet from equipment edge) not provided on landscape details. Vegetation conflicts can damage infrastructure but are typically resolvable through design adjustments. The landscape plan (Sheets 31, 32, 33, 34) needs coordination with the electrical plan (Sheet 21) to verify proximity relationships and confirm species compatibility with utility clearance requirements.\n\nWater, wastewater, and storm drain clearances require verification of utility line routing shown on utility plans, but horizontal clearances from transformer pads are not dimensioned. The minimum 5-foot separation required to water, wastewater, and storm drain utilities is not documented. Water service lines appear on the utility plan (Sheet 19) but lack dimensioned clearances from transformer pads on the electrical plan (Sheet 21). Wastewater line routing similarly shows no dimensioned clearances from transformer pads and cleanouts. Storm drain clearances also need to be addressed.\n\nThis represents a moderate severity issue since utility conflicts can typically be resolved through plan revisions or coordination between departments. The Water Utility, Wastewater, and Stormwater departments should verify there are no conflicts between transformer locations and utility lines. A utility coordination table or cross-reference drawing comparing the electrical plan (Sheet 21) with utility plans (Sheets 12, 19) would help resolve these discrepancies.\n\nUnderground utility routing and separation distances from transformer pads lack documentation. Minimum horizontal and vertical clearances to underground utilities—12 inches for non-gas lines and 24-36 inches for gas depending on pressure—along with crossing relationships need verification. Gas line locations on utility plans haven't been verified, and minimum horizontal clearances of 24 inches for lines ≤60 psi or 36 inches for higher-pressure lines from transformer pads aren't documented. Underground utility routing for telecom, cable, and recreational facilities also requires clarification regarding separation standards and crossing procedures. Cross-departmental coordination with gas, telecom, cable, and reclaimed water providers is essential, and Austin Energy may require a design utility separation study. I need to confirm gas service availability and request comprehensive utility cross-sections documenting the coordination analysis per code requirements.\n\nOverhead structures and conductor clearances lack documentation. Vertical clearance paths above transformer locations and horizontal separations from overhead conductors aren't shown in cross-section drawings, which are needed to verify adequate spacing and radial separation from utility lines.\n\nIf overhead distribution lines are present on adjacent properties or streets, the site plan doesn't document the required 7.5-foot minimum vertical clearance above all transformer pads and structures. Additionally, if primary or neutral conductors exist nearby, dimensioned radii showing minimum 15-foot separation from conductor centerlines (not pole centerlines) aren't provided for transformer pads and structures. Cross-section drawings are missing that would show vertical clearances from grade to overhead structures, roofs, overhangs, and utility lines.\n\nThe plan also lacks confirmation that no buildings, parking garages, poles, signs, antennas, tanks, or other facilities are positioned under or over any overhead primary or neutral conductors if such lines exist on adjacent properties. These overhead clearance issues present electrocution and equipment damage risks, though they're typically addressable through design modifications. The confidence level is low to moderate since only run 3 flagged most of these details, and the presence of adjacent overhead lines isn't fully confirmed.\n\nAustin Energy Design needs to verify whether overhead distribution lines exist on adjacent properties or streets, and if they do, provide utility coordination documentation confirming required clearances are maintained.\n\nTransformer pad locations relative to property boundaries and recorded easements lack explicit documentation. There's no confirmation that all pads and electrical routing are entirely on private property and outside any recorded easements.\n\nSpecific gaps include: distances from transformer pads to property boundaries are not dimensioned on the electrical plan, leaving unclear whether pads straddle property lines; transformer positions relative to recorded drainage, water, sewer, and overhead electric easements have not been verified, and if any pad falls within a prohibited easement, Austin Energy Design written approval is missing; transformer easement boundaries are not depicted on the site plan for most pads (only one labeled as 'Vault Easement'), and the required easement width and accessibility requirements have not been coordinated with Austin Energy Design; electrical routing from transformer pads to meters lacks verification that it remains entirely on private property, with no dimension callouts or notations confirming the routing avoids the public right-of-way. These issues carry moderate severity since property and easement violations can lead to plan rejection or enforcement action, though they're typically addressable through design modifications or obtaining necessary approvals from Austin Energy Design, and may require title company or surveyor verification of easement boundaries.\n\nThe plan documentation should cross-reference the property plat on Sheet 2 and site plans on Sheets 9 and 21 to confirm all pad and routing locations, along with easement instrument numbers and a compliance table.\n\nAccess door orientation and confined-space installation requirements for the transformer are not documented, including hot-stick operation clearance and niche specifications such as vertical clearance, fire rating, and ventilation.\n\nThe transformer access door orientation isn't identified on the electrical plan, and dimensioned clearances from the access-door sides to open areas or buildings (minimum 10-foot for hot-stick use area) aren't documented for all pads. Additionally, if any transformer sits in a confined space or niche configuration (enclosed on 3+ sides), cross-section drawings showing 35-foot vertical clearance inside the niche entrance and 3-hour fire-wall specifications are missing. Austin Energy Design must approve the transformer access configuration and clearances, while Architecture needs to confirm niche fire-rating compliance. The vault transformer near Building 8 requires cross-section drawings demonstrating 35-foot minimum vertical clearance within the niche and at its entrance if it's configured as a niche installation. Additionally, architectural documentation must confirm 3-hour fire-wall ratings and ventilation specifications, along with evidence of Austin Energy Design consultation—all currently absent from the submission.\n\nAustin Energy Design needs to clarify whether this is a niche or pad-mounted configuration, as the fire protection and ventilation requirements hinge on that determination. Architecture should provide the corresponding fire-wall and ventilation specifications if applicable. The critical next step is confirming with Austin Energy Design whether the transformer qualifies as a niche installation; if it does, these deficiencies block approval.\n\nTransformer pad specifications and equipment details are also missing, including dimensions, grading, surface drainage, and bollard protection for the parking-adjacent location. Austin Energy Design-approved pad details and equipment type specifications need to be submitted. areas, no dimension callouts or bollard detail sheets provided. Bollard specifications (4-inch minimum diameter galvanized rigid metal posts with equipment-door-opening spacing) not documented.\"\n\nThe severity is moderate since missing specifications and details require resubmission but may not prevent approval if provided promptly. Austin Energy Design needs to supply transformer pad details and service specifications, while site design must verify parking proximity and confirm bollard placement accordingly. The key action is obtaining transformer pad details from Austin Energy Design and determining whether any pads fall within 4 feet of parking areas—if they do, bollard specifications must align with AE Design equipment pad standards.\n\nNow I'm structuring this into the JSON schema with proper nesting of comments, details, and runComments fields to capture the complete review data including source citations and status tracking across review cycles.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3969ea08-252c-4d44-a662-db912565b571","uuid":"ea9c5c51-2162-4a47-a4e0-c3548f106ee1"}
{"level":30,"time":1778188186451,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"13.json","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR249H2RQ57590TFSDCTQQ0W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Looking at your 40 consolidated findings (all related to transformer pad electrical clearances), I'll now synthesize these into a focused set of comments at the discipline level (Utility - Electrical).\n\n**Discipline Scope:** This is purely **Tier 1 (Within Scope)** — all findings relate directly to Austin Energy (AE) transformer pad placement, clearance, and utility coordination requirements under UCM § 1.10 and ECM standards. This is the electrical utility discipline's core concern.\n\n**Consolidation Strategy:** Target ~10 comments for 40 findings (4:1 ratio). I'm grouping findings by regulatory similarity and corrective action scope:\n\n1. Building/structure proximity → 1 comment (6 findings)\n2. Site features & equipment spacing → 1 comment (9 findings)\n3. Vegetation & root protection → 1 comment (3 findings)\n4. Water/wastewater/storm utilities → 1 comment (3 findings)\n5. Underground & gas utilities → 1 comment (3 findings)\n6. Vertical clearances & overhead → 1 comment (4 findings)\n7. Property boundaries & easements → 1 comment (4 findings)\n8. Access & installation requirements → 1 comment (2 findings)\n9. Vault/niche configuration → 1 comment (2 findings)\n10. Pad specs, grading, protection → 1 comment (3 findings)\n\n**Total: 10 comments covering all 40 findings.**"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3969ea08-252c-4d44-a662-db912565b571","uuid":"eaa16796-4a7f-446b-a2a2-3cdf41010198"}
{"level":30,"time":1778188201771,"pid":122,"hostname":"13276d9b-948","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"2.json","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR249S03CKJKY1MYJ6K1SF43","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014jdpUpmkCaNSW3QUS59kf7","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"comments":[{"headline":"Trees violate distribution conductor clearance zones","summary":"Non-utility-compatible and large trees are positioned directly on or within prohibited clearance zones from overhead electric distribution conductors, with no documented Austin Energy approval for the placement.","sheets":[31,32,35],"applicableArea":"Southern property boundary along Valley View Road","severity":3,"confidence":3,"crossDep":"Austin Energy — clearance variance approval may be required","attn":"Critical safety hazard. Trees must be relocated beyond minimum clearance distances (15 feet for non-UC species; 25 feet for large trees) or documented Austin Energy approval must be obtained authorizing placement within restricted zones.","details":[{"text":"Non-utility-compatible species (Cedar Elm, Live Oak) are positioned within the 15-foot restricted zone adjacent to distribution conductors. No Austin Energy approval is documented.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.1","confidence":3,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"fail","comment":"Six mitigation trees shown directly on overhead electric line on landscape plan, with no clearance maintained from distribution conductor and no UC status documented for proposed species in plant schedule."},{"runNum":2,"status":"fail","comment":"Non-utility-compatible trees (Cedar Elm, Live Oak) are being planted within the 15-foot restricted zone adjacent to overhead electric distribution lines. No Austin Energy approval is documented."},{"runNum":3,"status":"fail","comment":"Non-utility-compatible trees (Cedar Elm, Live Oak) are positioned along the overhead electric distribution line boundary on the landscape plan without documented clearance verification or Austin Energy written approval. Provide documented Austin Energy approval authorizing these species within the restricted zone, or relocate trees to beyond 15 lateral feet from the outside conductor."}]},{"text":"Large trees (Cedar Elm 40–50 ft, Live Oak 50–80 ft, Hackberry 40–60 ft mature height per ECM Appendix F) are positioned within the 25-foot minimum clearance zone from distribution conductors.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.3","confidence":3,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"fail","comment":"Large trees (Live Oak and Cedar Elm with mature heights 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) shown directly on distribution OHE line on landscape plan, violating 25-foot minimum clearance from distribution conductors."},{"runNum":2,"status":"fail","comment":"Large trees (Cedar Elm and Live Oak, both 40+ feet mature height per ECM Appendix F) are proposed within 25 feet of overhead electric distribution lines without documented clearance verification or Austin Energy approval."},{"runNum":3,"status":"fail","comment":"Large trees (Cedar Elm at 40-50 feet, Live Oak at 50-80 feet, Hackberry at 40-60 feet mature height) are positioned along the overhead electric distribution line without documented clearance verification or Austin Energy approval. Provide clearance calculations or documented Austin Energy approval for trees within 25 feet of the distribution conductor, or relocate large trees to beyond 25 lateral feet from the outside conductor."}]},{"text":"Six mitigation trees are shown directly on the overhead electric line with no documented written Austin Energy approval authorizing species, locations, or sizes within restricted zones.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.15","confidence":3,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"fail","comment":"Six trees positioned directly on overhead electric line without documented written approval from Austin Energy authorizing the clearance variance."},{"runNum":2,"status":"fail","comment":"Trees are proposed within prohibited clearance zones (non-UC Cedar Elm and Live Oak within 15 feet of overhead distribution line). No documented Austin Energy written approval is provided. Applicant must obtain explicit written approval from Austin Energy or modify tree species/locations to comply with clearance requirements."},{"runNum":3,"status":"fail","comment":"Trees are positioned within prohibited electrical clearance zones without documented written approval from Austin Energy. Obtain and submit explicit written approval from Austin Energy authorizing the proposed tree placement (species, locations, and sizes) within restricted zones, or relocate all trees to comply with minimum clearance distances (15 feet from distribution conductors for non-utility-compatible trees, 25 feet for large trees, 10 feet from utility poles)."}]},{"text":"Trees are positioned directly on the OHE distribution line indicating zero clearance; no clearance distances documented or dimensioned on the landscape plan.","citation":"Reviewer Convention","sourceId":"2:EL-2.14","confidence":1,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"fail","comment":"Trees are shown directly on overhead electric line, indicating zero clearance maintained from distribution conductor. No clearance distances documented or dimensioned."}]}]},{"headline":"Tree clearances from utility poles undocumented","summary":"Clearance distances between proposed trees and utility poles are not dimensioned on the landscape plan, preventing verification that trees meet the 10-foot minimum lateral clearance requirement from poles.","sheets":[21,31],"applicableArea":"Southern property boundary","severity":2,"confidence":2,"crossDep":"","attn":"Run 3 escalated status from 'not-verifiable' to 'fail,' indicating trees are positioned along OHE boundary where poles are located. Dimension utility pole locations on landscape plan and document clearances demonstrating minimum 10 lateral feet from each pole.","details":[{"text":"Utility pole locations not clearly dimensioned on landscape plan relative to proposed tree locations. Trees are positioned along the OHE line boundary where utility poles are located; provide clearance documentation demonstrating at least 10 lateral feet from each pole.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.6","confidence":2,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"Utility poles exist on site but clearance distances from proposed trees to poles are not dimensioned or documented on landscape plan."},{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"Utility pole locations are not clearly dimensioned on the landscape plan relative to proposed tree locations. Clearance verification cannot be completed from available documentation."},{"runNum":3,"status":"fail","comment":"Trees are positioned along the OHE line boundary where utility poles are located. Provide clearance documentation demonstrating all trees are at least 10 lateral feet from each pole, or relocate trees to meet this clearance requirement."}]}]},{"headline":"Underground electric equipment clearances not documented","summary":"Distances between proposed trees and underground electrical equipment (pull boxes, vaults, pad-mounted transformers) are not dimensioned or overlaid on the landscape plan, preventing verification of required clearances. Root barrier details are not provided for trees within 20 feet of underground equipment.","sheets":[21,31,37],"applicableArea":"Utilities & Infrastructure","severity":2,"confidence":2,"crossDep":"","attn":"Overlay underground equipment locations from electrical plan (Sheet 21) onto landscape plan (Sheet 31). For any trees within 20 feet of underground equipment, provide root barrier details on landscape detail sheets (minimum 4-foot depth, 5-foot offset from equipment), or document that all trees are beyond 20 feet from underground equipment.","details":[{"text":"Underground electric equipment locations from the electrical plan (Sheet 21) are not shown on the landscape plan (Sheet 31). Clearance dimensions from proposed and retained trees to all underground equipment are not provided.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.8","confidence":3,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"Distances between proposed trees and underground electrical equipment not documented on plans."},{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"Specific locations of underground electrical equipment are not dimensioned relative to tree placements. Clearance verification cannot be determined from available documentation."},{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"Underground electric equipment locations from electrical plan (Sheet 21) are not shown on landscape plan (Sheet 31). Provide landscape plan with underground equipment (pull boxes, vaults) overlaid and labeled, with clearance dimensions from proposed and retained trees to all underground equipment, or add dimension callouts demonstrating trees are at least 5 lateral feet from all underground equipment."}]},{"text":"Pad-mounted equipment locations are not dimensioned relative to proposed tree placements on the landscape plan. Clearance verification cannot be completed from available documentation.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.7","confidence":1,"runComments":[{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"Pad-mounted equipment locations are not dimensioned relative to proposed tree placements on the landscape plan. Clearance verification cannot be completed from available documentation."}]},{"text":"Trees within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment are not identified on the landscape plan. Root barrier specifications (minimum 4-foot depth, 5-foot offset from equipment) are not provided on landscape detail sheets.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.9","confidence":3,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"Root barrier specifications, depths, and positions not documented for trees within 20 feet of underground electrical equipment."},{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"Trees within 20 feet of underground electrical equipment are not identified on the landscape plan, and root barrier specifications for such locations are not shown on landscape details."},{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"For any trees proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment, provide root barrier details on a landscape detail sheet specifying minimum 4-foot depth and 5-foot offset from equipment. Alternatively, provide landscape plan documentation showing all trees are beyond 20 feet from underground equipment."}]}]},{"headline":"Tree species and sizes not differentiated on plans","summary":"Large trees (mature height ≥40 feet) are not visually distinguished on the landscape plan, and tree species are not keyed by legend or labeled directly on the plan. The plant schedule lists installation heights only, not mature heights, making it impossible to identify which trees are subject to large-tree clearance restrictions.","sheets":[31,35],"applicableArea":"Landscape plan","severity":1,"confidence":2,"crossDep":"","attn":"Adding mature height notes to the plant schedule and using distinct symbols or notation on the landscape plan to identify large trees (Cedar Elm ~40–50 ft, Live Oak ~50–80 ft, Hackberry ~40–60 ft) will facilitate verification of electrical clearance compliance.","details":[{"text":"Large trees (Cedar Elm ~40–50 ft, Live Oak ~50–80 ft, Hackberry ~40–60 ft per ECM Appendix F) are not differentiated on the landscape plan or identified in the plant schedule by mature height, only by installation size (16' minimum).","citation":"Reviewer Convention","sourceId":"2:EL-2.11","confidence":3,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"fail","comment":"Live Oak and Cedar Elm are large trees (40+ feet mature height per ECM Appendix F) but plant schedule does not differentiate or identify them as large trees; only planting size (16' minimum) is documented."},{"runNum":2,"status":"fail","comment":"Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) are not visually differentiated on the landscape plan. Plant schedule lists installation heights (16'), not mature heights, making it difficult to identify trees subject to large-tree clearance restrictions."},{"runNum":3,"status":"fail","comment":"Large trees (mature height ≥40 feet) are not differentiated on the landscape plan. Add a note to the plant schedule specifying mature height for each proposed tree species, and use a distinct symbol or notation on the landscape plan to identify large trees (Cedar Elm ~40-50 ft, Live Oak ~50-80 ft, Hackberry ~40-60 ft) to allow verification of electrical clearance compliance."}]},{"text":"Trees proposed near electric facilities on the landscape plan are not identified by species using a keyed legend; species identification requires manual cross-reference to a separate plant list rather than direct labeling on the plan.","citation":"Reviewer Convention","sourceId":"2:EL-2.10","confidence":1,"runComments":[{"runNum":2,"status":"fail","comment":"Trees proposed near electric facilities on the landscape plan are not identified by species using a keyed legend. Species identification requires manual cross-reference to a separate plant list rather than direct labeling on the plan."}]}]},{"headline":"Clearance distances from trees not dimensioned","summary":"No dimension lines are provided on the landscape plan showing clearances from trees to overhead electric conductors. Measurement reference points (trunk center vs. canopy edge) and measurement methodology cannot be verified from available documentation.","sheets":[21,31],"applicableArea":"Landscape plan trees near overhead electric line","severity":2,"confidence":2,"crossDep":"","attn":"Add dimension lines to the landscape plan showing measured clearances from tree trunks to nearest conductor or pole. Specify whether measurements are taken from trunk center or from canopy perimeter edge, and confirm all clearances meet minimum standards per UCM § 1.10.10.4.","details":[{"text":"No clearance dimensions are provided on landscape plan showing tree-to-conductor distances. Measurement methodology and reference points cannot be verified from the plans.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.12","confidence":2,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"No clearance dimensions provided on plans; measurement methodology cannot be verified."},{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"No dimension lines are provided on the landscape plan showing tree clearances from overhead electric conductors. Measurement methodology cannot be verified."}]},{"text":"No dimension lines show whether tree clearances were measured from tree trunk centers or from canopy edges. Reference point methodology cannot be verified from available documentation.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.13","confidence":2,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"No clearance dimensions provided on landscape plan; measurement reference point (trunk vs. canopy) cannot be verified."},{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"No dimension lines on the landscape plan show whether tree clearances were measured from tree trunk centers or canopy edges. Measurement methodology cannot be verified from available documentation."}]}]},{"headline":"Voltage classification of overhead electric lines not specified","summary":"The voltage classification of existing overhead electric lines (transmission ≥60 kV vs. distribution <60 kV) is not specified on the plans. Without this information, compliance with applicable clearance requirements cannot be verified.","sheets":[21,31,35],"applicableArea":"Southern and western property boundaries","severity":2,"confidence":1,"crossDep":"Austin Energy — voltage classification confirmation required","attn":"Obtain from Austin Energy or the electrical utility the voltage classification for all existing overhead electric lines on or adjacent to the property. If transmission-class (≥60 kV), verify 50-foot clearance; if distribution (<60 kV), verify appropriate distribution clearances per UCM § 1.10.10.4.","details":[{"text":"Voltage classification of overhead electric lines is not specified on plans (Sheets 21, 31). Cannot determine if transmission (≥60 kV) or distribution (<60 kV) classification applies to verify 50-foot transmission clearance compliance.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.2","confidence":1,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"Voltage classification of existing overhead electric lines not specified on plans. Cannot determine if transmission (≥60kV) or distribution (<60kV) classification applies, and therefore cannot verify compliance with 50-foot transmission clearance requirement."}]},{"text":"Voltage classification not specified on plans. Cannot independently verify transmission clearance compliance without confirmation that the overhead electric line is transmission-class (≥60 kV).","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.4","confidence":1,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"Voltage classification not specified. Cannot independently verify transmission clearance compliance without confirmation that OHE is transmission-class (≥60kV)."}]}]}]}}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ccfa398d-4214-4f81-b393-ac32991500b5","uuid":"de0b4cf1-1b5b-4170-bdcf-2e4adfe99fca"}
