nces. The second group could be \"Utility Infrastructure & Equipment\" since it's about utility placement and spacing requirements.\n\nLooking at the next section, I need to complete the partial thought about trees and landscaping. The items here involve utility coordination, building proximity documentation, equipment spacing, and various utility separations. These fit better under infrastructure and utilities rather than a separate landscaping category. The transformer-related items (13-8, 13-9) and underground/gas separation (13-4) are critical infrastructure concerns. Property boundaries and easements (13-6) also relate to utility placement constraints. This group emphasizes utility equipment placement, spacing requirements, and coordination between different utility systems. Looking at the applicable areas and primary focus of each comment, I'm reconsidering how to group these. Several comments have \"Plan Documentation\" listed, including the construction safety clearances, property boundary verification, and tree species documentation. These seem like they belong in a documentation-focused section rather than being split across multiple categories.\n\nThe utility routing and easement verification comment is primarily about infrastructure planning, while the tree documentation is about landscape plan details. But since both relate to plan completeness and verification, grouping them under plan documentation makes sense as a catch-all for items spanning multiple disciplines or requiring documentation review.\n\nLet me reorganize by identifying the core focus areas: building and site clearances (setbacks, elevations, fencing), infrastructure and utilities (electrical, water, gas, transformers), and street trees with landscaping. Within each section, I'll order by severity and confidence to prioritize the most critical and certain issues.\n\nFor building and site clearances, the highest priority items are building elevation/conductor clearances and driveway clearances (both severity 3, confidence 3), followed by fence and retaining wall clearances. For infrastructure, building proximity documentation and underground/gas separation are most critical (severity 3), with transformer-related items and utility coordination following. Street trees and landscaping starts with overhead clearance violations (severity 3, confidence 3), then tree species compatibility and other landscape considerations.\n\nSince all three sections contain severity 3 issues, I'm ordering by the count of critical items: infrastructure has three severity 3 comments, while building clearances and street trees each have two, making infrastructure the priority section to address first. 2-0 (confidence 3)\n\nSection 2 has the most severity-3 comments with three total, so it should come first. Between Section 1 and Section 3, both have two severity-3 comments at confidence 3, but Section 3 contains more issues overall with eight comments versus five. I'll order them as: Section 2 (Infrastructure & Utilities), then Section 3 (Street Trees & Landscaping), then Section 1.\n\nLooking back at the source data, the comment series appear as 1-x, 13-x, then 2-x. Since all sections max out at severity 3, I'm using the count of highest-severity comments as the primary tiebreaker, with total issue volume as secondary.\n\nThe logical dependency chain suggests utilities should be addressed first (foundational infrastructure), followed by building clearances (which depend on utility placement), then landscaping (which must account for both). However, the instructions specify severity-based ordering, so I'll stick with that approach.\n\nLet me verify the comment assignments are correct by checking what each one actually addresses.\n\n**Section 1: Building & Site Clearances** covers all the clearance requirements—building elevations, driveways, fences, retaining walls, and vertical clearances from overhead conductors. These all relate to how site elements maintain safe distanc