tion requirement—ensuring construction plans include proper safety and utility information. Given that I have 25 total comments and want to maintain 4-10 sections, creating a dedicated Plan Documentation & Administrative section with this single comment seems justified if it prevents the comment from being forced into a misaligned category. I'll add it as Section 4 with severity and confidence both at 3. a stretch for \"Fire Access & Life Safety\".\n\nLet me step back and look at the actual patterns in the comments. The dominant themes are overhead conductor clearances spanning multiple comment numbers, utility equipment and infrastructure concerns, and then trees and landscaping issues. Reorganizing around these core themes might make more sense than forcing comments into predetermined sections.\n\nThe real challenge is that these comments touch several different disciplines—electrical coordination, landscape design, civil engineering, and project documentation all intersect here. If I organize by what's physically involved rather than by comment number, I could group around building elements needing clearances from overhead lines, vegetation management with proper documentation, utility placement and specifications, and the safety information that needs to be included throughout. This approach would naturally consolidate the overlapping comments and create clearer, more functional sections.\n\nLooking at the distribution, I'd have a substantial section on infrastructure and utilities with 11 comments, another on street trees and landscaping with 8, building and site clearances with 5, and just 1 comment on documentation. That last section feels too thin. Comment 1-8 about construction safety clearances and utility notification is really about ensuring contractors know where utilities are located—that's fundamentally a utility coordination issue, so it belongs in the infrastructure section rather than standing alone.\n\nMerging 1-8 into Section 2 gives me 12 comments there. Now I need to prioritize them by severity and confidence level, starting with the most critical issues.\n\nFor severity 3, I have four comments: 13-0 and 1-8 both at confidence 3, then 13-4 at confidence 2, and 13-8 at confidence 1. The severity 2 group is larger—1-5, 1-6, and 1-7 all at confidence 2, while 13-1, 13-3, 13-6, 13-7, and 13-9 are all at confidence 3. I'll rank the higher-confidence items first within each severity tier.\n\nArranging by severity then confidence gives me: 13-0 and 1-8 (both 3,3), then 13-4 (3,2), then 13-8 (3,1), followed by the five confidence-3 severity-2 items in their original sequence, then the three confidence-2 severity-2 items. This produces the complete ordering for Section 2. Moving to Section 1, the priority comments are 1-0, 1-1, 1-2, 1-3, and 13-5, which address critical clearance requirements for the building envelope.\n\nFor Section 3, the eight comments ordered by severity and confidence are 1-4, 2-0, 13-2, 2-1, 2-2, 2-4, 2-5, 2-3, covering tree species selection and clearance documentation from electrical lines and underground utilities with dimensional and voltage specifications. Since Section 2 has four severity-3 comments while Sections 1 and 3 each have two, Section 2 ranks highest, with Sections 1 and 3 following in that order. -8\", \"13-4\", \"13-8\", \"13-1\", \"13-3\", \"13-6\", \"13-7\", \"13-9\", \"1-5\", \"1-6\", \"1-7\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"label\": \"Building Design & Setbacks\",\n      \"summary\": \"Critical clearance requirements for building envelope, driveways, and site structures from overhead electrical conductors must be documented with surveyed elevations and measurements.\",\n      \"commentIds\": [\"1-0\", \"1-1\", \"1-2\", \"1-3\", \"13-5\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"label\": \"Street Trees & Landscaping\",\n      \"summary\": \"Tree species and clearance documentation requirements from overhead electrical lines and underground utilities must be clarified with dimensional specificati